WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.207 --> 00:00:12.216
- We will proceed as
we did in the morning,

00:00:12.216 --> 00:00:14.248
in alphabetical order.

00:00:14.248 --> 00:00:17.725
So I'll just introduce
each of the panelists now

00:00:17.725 --> 00:00:19.526
and then get going.

00:00:19.526 --> 00:00:22.135
Our first speaker will be John Bender

00:00:22.135 --> 00:00:23.093
who is Professor of English

00:00:23.093 --> 00:00:25.208
and Comparative Literature at Stanford

00:00:25.208 --> 00:00:28.332
and former Director of the
Stanford Humanities Center.

00:00:28.332 --> 00:00:30.041
And author, most recently

00:00:30.041 --> 00:00:33.583
of "The Culture of Diagram"
with Michael Marrinan,

00:00:33.583 --> 00:00:36.075
and "Ends of Enlightenment."

00:00:36.075 --> 00:00:38.218
And then we will hear from Sophie Gee

00:00:38.218 --> 00:00:41.489
who is Associate Professor
of English at Princeton.

00:00:41.489 --> 00:00:43.833
She's the author of "Making Waste"

00:00:43.833 --> 00:00:46.333
and "The Scandal of the Season,"

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:50.601
and is currently finishing a book entitled

00:00:50.601 --> 00:00:54.351
"Infidelity: The Rise of
the Sacrificial Novel."

00:00:56.314 --> 00:00:58.766
We will then hear from Sarah Tindal Kareem

00:00:58.766 --> 00:01:02.529
who is Associate Professor
of English at UCLA.

00:01:02.529 --> 00:01:05.934
Her current research
focuses on how the metaphors

00:01:05.934 --> 00:01:09.650
18th-century writers used to
talk about reading fiction,

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:11.205
in particular the metaphors

00:01:11.205 --> 00:01:13.018
of entering into a fictional world

00:01:13.018 --> 00:01:15.386
or following a narrative thread.

00:01:15.386 --> 00:01:19.553
Reflect and afford distinct
modes of readerly attachment.

00:01:21.424 --> 00:01:23.280
Wendy Lee is Assistant Professor

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.750
in the English Department at NYU

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:29.605
where she teaches 18th-century
Literature and Philosophy.

00:01:29.605 --> 00:01:31.644
She is finishing a book that she says

00:01:31.644 --> 00:01:34.732
with appropriate laconic quality

00:01:34.732 --> 00:01:38.232
about the origins of the Bartleby problem.

00:01:39.721 --> 00:01:42.898
And finally last but not
least, Courtney Weiss Smith

00:01:42.898 --> 00:01:46.840
is Associate Professor
of English at Wesleyan.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:50.275
And her recent book, "Empiricist Devotions:

00:01:50.275 --> 00:01:54.037
"Science, Religion and Poetry
in Early 18th-century England"

00:01:54.037 --> 00:01:55.635
won the Cowen Prize.

00:01:55.635 --> 00:01:56.468
- Yay.

00:01:56.468 --> 00:01:57.329
- So yay.

00:01:57.329 --> 00:01:59.496
With no further ado, John.

00:02:01.479 --> 00:02:05.219
- I had a title and I'll explain
it to you as we go along.

00:02:05.219 --> 00:02:08.644
It's "Foley Effects in the Gothic:

00:02:08.644 --> 00:02:10.903
"Sound in 'The Castle of Otranto.'"

00:02:10.903 --> 00:02:13.133
And this handout's
going around, I'll wait.

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:15.148
Don't start my ten minutes yet Ruth.

00:02:15.148 --> 00:02:17.981
(everyone laughs)

00:02:26.565 --> 00:02:30.160
So a few modern critics have written

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.327
specifically about sound in
the Gothic novel, but very few.

00:02:37.233 --> 00:02:40.854
But noises were considered
strong generic markers

00:02:40.854 --> 00:02:44.271
of the Gothic in the 18th century itself.

00:02:46.087 --> 00:02:47.830
Three different periodicals of the time.

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:52.363
And I owe these references
to Tasha Eccles, who's here.

00:02:52.363 --> 00:02:56.401
Offer profiles of the
Gothic, and they insist

00:02:56.401 --> 00:03:00.408
as say did, the spirit of
the public journals in 1798.

00:03:00.408 --> 00:03:02.472
That the Gothic required, and I quote,

00:03:02.472 --> 00:03:05.191
"Noises, whispers and groans

00:03:05.191 --> 00:03:09.452
"and indeed demanded three
score at least, of each."

00:03:09.452 --> 00:03:12.057
And other profiles add things

00:03:12.057 --> 00:03:15.280
like whistles, wind, rushes, howls, rain,

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:18.197
creaking doors and clanking chains.

00:03:19.103 --> 00:03:23.424
So the early inclusion of
sound as a marker of the Gothic

00:03:23.424 --> 00:03:25.923
works to confirm my intuition

00:03:25.923 --> 00:03:29.316
that sounds are less
prominent in what we can call

00:03:29.316 --> 00:03:33.447
standard English novels of
the period than in the Gothic.

00:03:33.447 --> 00:03:37.126
And indeed for the 19th century,
the Lit labs at Stanford

00:03:37.126 --> 00:03:40.518
has had a project which seems to show

00:03:40.518 --> 00:03:42.409
that across the 19th century,

00:03:42.409 --> 00:03:45.058
novels become less loud and noisy

00:03:45.058 --> 00:03:49.501
with a decline of words
like shriek, cried, screamed

00:03:49.501 --> 00:03:51.334
and other such things.

00:03:52.404 --> 00:03:56.571
Conversely, when I opened
"Otranto" to prepare this talk

00:03:57.809 --> 00:04:02.489
I was overwhelmed by the
profusion of sonic dimensions

00:04:02.489 --> 00:04:05.739
in the text's beginnings and thereafter.

00:04:06.818 --> 00:04:10.985
The eruptions of sound everywhere
and also marked silences.

00:04:13.388 --> 00:04:16.825
Now this encounter brings
me to Foley effects.

00:04:16.825 --> 00:04:21.026
One very prominent kind
of sound effect in film.

00:04:21.026 --> 00:04:23.711
So what are Foley effects?

00:04:23.711 --> 00:04:27.653
Like virtually every sound
in a film you attend,

00:04:27.653 --> 00:04:32.576
other than dialogue, they're
laid down on a soundtrack

00:04:32.576 --> 00:04:35.553
after the shooting of the film.

00:04:35.553 --> 00:04:38.359
Only dialogue is recorded generally.

00:04:38.359 --> 00:04:41.541
They're superimposed by the invisible hand

00:04:41.541 --> 00:04:43.785
of the Foley artist.

00:04:43.785 --> 00:04:47.904
I'm viewing Walpole here
as an unseen sound artist

00:04:47.904 --> 00:04:51.775
who calls attention to crucial noises.

00:04:51.775 --> 00:04:55.400
And Walpole sounds, like those in films

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:58.855
are highly selective and
very, very importantly

00:04:58.855 --> 00:05:01.163
louder than in real life.

00:05:01.163 --> 00:05:04.462
That those glasses
clinking, the lock latching,

00:05:04.462 --> 00:05:07.514
the footsteps creaking,
they're always louder.

00:05:07.514 --> 00:05:10.146
There are exceptions and I'll
mention those in a minute.

00:05:10.146 --> 00:05:14.146
So he simulates a sense
of real sonic experience

00:05:16.138 --> 00:05:19.555
using the unreal device of printed words.

00:05:21.739 --> 00:05:25.739
Michel Chion says of film,
great sound theorist.

00:05:28.181 --> 00:05:31.133
He says the realest art of cinema

00:05:31.133 --> 00:05:35.687
has progressed only by straining
against its own principle

00:05:35.687 --> 00:05:38.770
through forceful doses of un-realism.

00:05:39.839 --> 00:05:43.509
And I would say that
"Otranto" fits this as well.

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:46.273
The point being that what we
perceive as realism in film

00:05:46.273 --> 00:05:49.023
is actually very stylized indeed.

00:05:50.518 --> 00:05:52.582
It's a series of simulations.

00:05:52.582 --> 00:05:56.325
Now Foley sounds are
made with real objects

00:05:56.325 --> 00:05:59.151
but disconnected from the real.

00:05:59.151 --> 00:06:00.818
They're simulations.

00:06:04.670 --> 00:06:06.494
Foley effects and other sounds effects

00:06:06.494 --> 00:06:10.661
they achieve realism, but often
by employing the not real.

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:14.971
A famous instance in the literature

00:06:14.971 --> 00:06:18.004
on sound and film, is snow.

00:06:18.004 --> 00:06:21.135
Real snow does not sound like snow.

00:06:21.135 --> 00:06:23.590
You have to use cornstarch.

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:27.598
And real silk stockings don't
sound like silk stockings,

00:06:27.598 --> 00:06:29.716
you have to use Nylon.

00:06:29.716 --> 00:06:32.267
And there are countless other examples.

00:06:32.267 --> 00:06:35.901
I'll mention some of them along the way.

00:06:35.901 --> 00:06:39.434
The great sound engineer Walter
Murch made this observation

00:06:39.434 --> 00:06:43.524
and it's been picked up
widely in the film criticism.

00:06:43.524 --> 00:06:46.949
So this kind of realism, like I would say

00:06:46.949 --> 00:06:50.745
realism in a novel in general,
does not equal the real.

00:06:50.745 --> 00:06:54.912
So Foley effects almost always
imply the spectral therefore

00:06:56.048 --> 00:06:58.798
because they come out of nowhere.

00:07:00.314 --> 00:07:04.481
Chion, again has this term,
the audio visual contract.

00:07:06.188 --> 00:07:09.393
And I think that's very
much at work in "Otranto."

00:07:09.393 --> 00:07:12.817
The idea is that when we see a film.

00:07:12.817 --> 00:07:17.025
If we just saw the image, or
we just heard the soundtrack

00:07:17.025 --> 00:07:18.887
it would be ineffective.

00:07:18.887 --> 00:07:21.094
There's a audio visual contract

00:07:21.094 --> 00:07:23.384
in which we witness them together

00:07:23.384 --> 00:07:27.204
and they have this magical, realistic,

00:07:27.204 --> 00:07:30.007
generally realistic character.

00:07:30.007 --> 00:07:34.174
So there's a synergistic
co-presence of image and sound.

00:07:35.769 --> 00:07:38.532
So what are the traits of Foley effects?

00:07:38.532 --> 00:07:41.808
They're actual sounds on soundtracks

00:07:41.808 --> 00:07:44.185
like locks, boards, floorboards,

00:07:44.185 --> 00:07:46.362
clinking glasses, folding sheet metal.

00:07:46.362 --> 00:07:48.386
So sometimes they're actual sounds

00:07:48.386 --> 00:07:50.793
of things that may appear in the film.

00:07:50.793 --> 00:07:52.675
They're almost always louder

00:07:52.675 --> 00:07:55.342
than they would be in real life.

00:07:56.931 --> 00:08:00.848
Now silence in real life
never is truly silent.

00:08:03.145 --> 00:08:05.318
You hear that in the background here.

00:08:05.318 --> 00:08:08.455
So if you were making a film of this room.

00:08:08.455 --> 00:08:10.583
Your Foley artist would record that sound

00:08:10.583 --> 00:08:15.063
and lay it on the track
as the sound of this room.

00:08:15.063 --> 00:08:19.790
Foley puts all of those
kinds of sounds on film.

00:08:19.790 --> 00:08:22.603
Street noise, the noises of a hotel,

00:08:22.603 --> 00:08:26.770
doors closing, people
talking, TVs on and so forth.

00:08:29.983 --> 00:08:31.718
But still, the differentiation between

00:08:31.718 --> 00:08:34.954
silence and sound effects
is very important in film.

00:08:34.954 --> 00:08:39.335
And a very famous example
is this extremely short

00:08:39.335 --> 00:08:42.705
total silence in "Godfather II" at the end

00:08:42.705 --> 00:08:44.473
when Michael is sitting by the lake

00:08:44.473 --> 00:08:46.350
having had his brother murdered.

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:50.517
And the sound slowly goes
down to absolute silence.

00:08:52.751 --> 00:08:56.918
Novels can give us absolute
silence and "Otranto" does that.

00:08:58.721 --> 00:09:00.438
And this may be the moment to mention

00:09:00.438 --> 00:09:04.605
Robert Barsund's assertion that
with sound coming into film

00:09:06.206 --> 00:09:09.261
silence in film became possible.

00:09:09.261 --> 00:09:11.256
Now silent films weren't really silent.

00:09:11.256 --> 00:09:13.290
I mean there was orchestras
and all sorts of things.

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:16.229
But, you can't have one without the other.

00:09:16.229 --> 00:09:20.396
You can't have sound effects,
silence without sound effects.

00:09:24.181 --> 00:09:27.398
Now, sound effects in film in general

00:09:27.398 --> 00:09:29.568
are aimed at the audience.

00:09:29.568 --> 00:09:32.545
But there are exceptions in sound effects

00:09:32.545 --> 00:09:36.045
that render psycho effects of a character.

00:09:36.928 --> 00:09:39.719
Very common in horror
films and we see that.

00:09:39.719 --> 00:09:41.743
I'll show you in a minute in "Otranto"

00:09:41.743 --> 00:09:45.776
with Isabella down in the
subterranean corridor.

00:09:45.776 --> 00:09:47.730
So having told you something about Foley

00:09:47.730 --> 00:09:50.897
I'm gonna show you the making of Foley

00:09:52.226 --> 00:09:55.774
for a short film sequence, police chase.

00:09:55.774 --> 00:09:58.191
On your left will be the film

00:09:59.726 --> 00:10:02.599
and on the right will
be the Foley artists.

00:10:02.599 --> 00:10:04.961
And notice that sometimes
they're doing what's happening

00:10:04.961 --> 00:10:07.760
and sometimes they're doing
something completely different.

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:10.590
This is gonna be two minutes.

00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:12.173
You're on Michelle.

00:10:24.249 --> 00:10:26.327
- Foley is the process
of creating sound effects

00:10:26.327 --> 00:10:30.494
live in a studio along with
prerecorded piece of video.

00:10:35.135 --> 00:10:37.390
Foley artists use a variety of props

00:10:37.390 --> 00:10:38.815
to create or replace sounds

00:10:38.815 --> 00:10:42.148
that cannot properly be captured on set.

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:47.363
Here, you see two Hollywood Foley artists

00:10:47.363 --> 00:10:50.363
bringing an action sequence to life.

00:10:51.556 --> 00:10:53.655
(audience laughs)

00:10:53.655 --> 00:10:56.537
(sound of clattering items)

00:10:56.537 --> 00:11:00.037
(sound of glass smashing)

00:11:22.435 --> 00:11:24.423
(sound of clattering items)

00:11:24.423 --> 00:11:27.256
(audience laughs)

00:11:32.256 --> 00:11:35.826
(audience laughs)

00:11:35.826 --> 00:11:39.243
(sound of items banging)

00:11:40.518 --> 00:11:43.351
(audience laughs)

00:11:47.665 --> 00:11:49.592
(sound of items falling)

00:11:49.592 --> 00:11:52.425
(audience laughs)

00:11:55.132 --> 00:11:58.549
(sound of items falling)

00:12:07.590 --> 00:12:11.090
(sound of a pipe falling)

00:12:16.832 --> 00:12:19.165
- I think we can stop there.

00:12:21.117 --> 00:12:23.660
You're missing the part where the cop,

00:12:23.660 --> 00:12:26.130
where the thief punches the cop.

00:12:26.130 --> 00:12:27.495
And this is an example of

00:12:27.495 --> 00:12:31.117
a different sound creating the real

00:12:31.117 --> 00:12:34.348
because what the Foley artists
punch, is a leg of lamb.

00:12:34.348 --> 00:12:36.743
(everyone laughs)

00:12:36.743 --> 00:12:39.939
Real punches don't make
much sound apparently

00:12:39.939 --> 00:12:44.366
and so you have to punch
something else to make the sound.

00:12:44.366 --> 00:12:47.168
So what we have here,
sometimes is they're doing

00:12:47.168 --> 00:12:49.950
what the character in the films are doing,

00:12:49.950 --> 00:12:54.117
other times they're making
some kind of artful substitute.

00:12:55.252 --> 00:12:59.419
And so they're producing a
realism by a high artificiality

00:13:02.123 --> 00:13:04.306
that substitutes alternatives very often

00:13:04.306 --> 00:13:07.108
for normal sounds that we would hear.

00:13:07.108 --> 00:13:10.014
Now let's look quickly at the handout.

00:13:10.014 --> 00:13:11.852
I'll just point out a
couple of things here.

00:13:11.852 --> 00:13:14.293
There are more highlighted
things than I can cover.

00:13:14.293 --> 00:13:15.126
I believe.

00:13:15.126 --> 00:13:18.709
But the first is the
famous rustling sound.

00:13:20.121 --> 00:13:25.107
The proportionable quantity
of plumes on the fatal helmet.

00:13:25.107 --> 00:13:26.660
This seems to me a perfect example

00:13:26.660 --> 00:13:29.158
of the audio visual contract.

00:13:29.158 --> 00:13:31.090
You have the plumes moving.

00:13:31.090 --> 00:13:35.257
Okay, without the rustling
sound, without that Foley effect

00:13:36.543 --> 00:13:40.126
it would not be the
vivid thing that it is.

00:13:42.048 --> 00:13:44.555
And then the pictures sighing.

00:13:44.555 --> 00:13:46.644
In some Gothic novels, pictures sigh

00:13:46.644 --> 00:13:51.356
and then step out of the frame,
which would be even better.

00:13:51.356 --> 00:13:54.230
And then once here, the second part,

00:13:54.230 --> 00:13:55.943
the lower part of the castle.

00:13:55.943 --> 00:13:58.381
I'm sure you're all
familiar with this flight

00:13:58.381 --> 00:14:02.290
of Isabella into the lower
chambers of the castle.

00:14:02.290 --> 00:14:05.806
And there's an awful silence there.

00:14:05.806 --> 00:14:07.925
Now that's a total novelistic silence.

00:14:07.925 --> 00:14:12.843
That's not the silence of
this room or a Foley silence.

00:14:12.843 --> 00:14:14.037
The novel can give that to us.

00:14:14.037 --> 00:14:17.288
But then blasts of wind shake the doors

00:14:17.288 --> 00:14:20.121
as she passed, rusty hinges creak.

00:14:21.659 --> 00:14:23.854
They're re-echoed down the labyrinth.

00:14:23.854 --> 00:14:26.085
So you can imagine how these Foley artists

00:14:26.085 --> 00:14:28.529
would create that echo.

00:14:28.529 --> 00:14:30.584
But then something happens.

00:14:30.584 --> 00:14:34.498
Every murmur struck her with new terror

00:14:34.498 --> 00:14:36.820
and the sounds move inside

00:14:36.820 --> 00:14:40.117
into phantasms and
imaginations of her head.

00:14:40.117 --> 00:14:43.534
On a film, those would still be rendered.

00:14:44.510 --> 00:14:48.655
But we would intuit through
the audio visual contract

00:14:48.655 --> 00:14:52.123
that we were now fusing
the sounds with her mind

00:14:52.123 --> 00:14:56.741
rather than with some external
object like a door hinge.

00:14:56.741 --> 00:15:00.907
Then we get the gust of wind,
the lamp, total darkness.

00:15:00.907 --> 00:15:03.107
If you, I think turn over.

00:15:03.107 --> 00:15:05.779
This is where Theodore appears.

00:15:05.779 --> 00:15:08.791
And the moonshine that
Jonathan mentioned earlier

00:15:08.791 --> 00:15:12.630
coming down and striking the trap door

00:15:12.630 --> 00:15:13.992
that they're looking for.

00:15:13.992 --> 00:15:16.825
She touches it, the spring clicks.

00:15:18.195 --> 00:15:19.883
Goes down in total darkness.

00:15:19.883 --> 00:15:23.716
Now total darkness is
also very hard to achieve

00:15:23.716 --> 00:15:27.605
in the real world, but you
can have it in the novel.

00:15:27.605 --> 00:15:30.161
It's very hard to achieve on film actually

00:15:30.161 --> 00:15:31.992
because then the image goes away

00:15:31.992 --> 00:15:34.020
and we just have a blank screen.

00:15:34.020 --> 00:15:36.331
So then she goes down
through the trap door.

00:15:36.331 --> 00:15:40.498
And I'll just close the discussion
of the handout with this.

00:15:42.105 --> 00:15:45.355
"Shut the trap door after you," she says.

00:15:46.190 --> 00:15:48.643
"He let the door slip out of his hands.

00:15:48.643 --> 00:15:51.810
"It fell and the spring closed over it."

00:15:53.608 --> 00:15:57.606
The noise, and now becomes diegetic again.

00:15:57.606 --> 00:16:01.926
The noise of the falling door
had been heard by Manfred.

00:16:01.926 --> 00:16:05.343
So now we take off and Manfred cries out.

00:16:08.254 --> 00:16:12.207
Okay, so this is basically my intuition

00:16:12.207 --> 00:16:16.858
that there's a kind of
special sound in "Otranto"

00:16:16.858 --> 00:16:20.275
that produces realism through the unreal.

00:16:22.752 --> 00:16:26.428
And I just note a few other
places in a Gothic novel

00:16:26.428 --> 00:16:30.346
where I think we could discuss
this, if we weren't doing the

00:16:30.346 --> 00:16:33.513
conference equivalent of speed dating.

00:16:34.850 --> 00:16:37.683
Various sequences in "Udolpho" where

00:16:38.691 --> 00:16:40.485
Valancourt is playing the lute.

00:16:40.485 --> 00:16:43.089
Tasha gave me that one.

00:16:43.089 --> 00:16:46.567
The odd lack of sound
at the end of "The Monk."

00:16:46.567 --> 00:16:47.801
When I was working on this, I thought

00:16:47.801 --> 00:16:50.465
oh, the end of "The Monk" will
be very noisy, but it's not.

00:16:50.465 --> 00:16:52.040
Very interesting.

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:53.589
Thrown voices in

00:16:53.589 --> 00:16:57.281
"Wieland" and "Confessions
of a Justified Sinner."

00:16:57.281 --> 00:17:01.281
Rochester's mysterious
cry to Jane in "Jane Eyre."

00:17:02.917 --> 00:17:06.151
The wind in "Wuthering Heights."

00:17:06.151 --> 00:17:10.571
And lots and lots of
sounds in Edgar Allan Poe.

00:17:10.571 --> 00:17:15.427
So it's just a kind of informal
intuition about the novel.

00:17:15.427 --> 00:17:19.550
I had a lot of fun reading
these great film theorists.

00:17:19.550 --> 00:17:22.808
I have one of Michel Chion's books here.

00:17:22.808 --> 00:17:24.604
And Walter Murch is only on the web

00:17:24.604 --> 00:17:26.304
but he's absolutely fantastic.

00:17:26.304 --> 00:17:28.785
He did the sound for the "Godfather" films

00:17:28.785 --> 00:17:31.941
and many other truly memorable films.

00:17:31.941 --> 00:17:33.504
So that's it.

00:17:33.504 --> 00:17:34.337
- Thank you.

00:17:34.337 --> 00:17:37.337
(audience clapping)

00:17:44.309 --> 00:17:45.705
- John you've got me thinking about

00:17:45.705 --> 00:17:48.500
a lot of different things but including

00:17:48.500 --> 00:17:52.667
what would be the speed dating
version of a conference.

00:17:54.361 --> 00:17:57.518
Maybe marriage, I don't know.

00:17:57.518 --> 00:17:58.679
(audience laughs)

00:17:58.679 --> 00:17:59.788
- Maybe divorce, you all leave.

00:17:59.788 --> 00:18:00.871
- Yeah, yeah.

00:18:02.824 --> 00:18:06.324
Okay so I want to talk with my ten minutes

00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:11.245
about authority, about inwardness

00:18:11.245 --> 00:18:14.312
and about how the two states coexist.

00:18:14.312 --> 00:18:18.010
With some passing references
to "The Castle of Otranto."

00:18:18.010 --> 00:18:20.225
And I'm gonna present this as a list

00:18:20.225 --> 00:18:23.033
with some detailed points.

00:18:23.033 --> 00:18:25.478
And I'm into lists at the moment

00:18:25.478 --> 00:18:28.728
because they don't require an argument.

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:33.407
So you can do what you like with them.

00:18:35.249 --> 00:18:37.082
Okay, so point one.

00:18:37.082 --> 00:18:41.800
So this is a very odd book
which relapses from time to time

00:18:41.800 --> 00:18:44.050
into other kinds of oddity.

00:18:46.057 --> 00:18:49.013
The sort of, quote
unquote "realistic spaces"

00:18:49.013 --> 00:18:52.275
that we would associate
with 18th-century places

00:18:52.275 --> 00:18:55.624
like the nursery, the boarding
school, the men's club.

00:18:55.624 --> 00:18:57.624
I think that these sites

00:18:58.736 --> 00:19:00.948
are sort of kicking around unacknowledged

00:19:00.948 --> 00:19:03.712
in "The Castle of Otranto."

00:19:03.712 --> 00:19:06.883
In these moments where the
dialogue really could go

00:19:06.883 --> 00:19:08.234
in either direction.

00:19:08.234 --> 00:19:09.745
It could belong in a realist novel

00:19:09.745 --> 00:19:12.909
or well it should belong
in a realist novel

00:19:12.909 --> 00:19:14.460
but instead it's in a Gothic novel.

00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:18.241
And I've chosen those sites advisably.

00:19:18.241 --> 00:19:20.741
So I'll tell you why in a minute.

00:19:20.741 --> 00:19:23.241
What we see in "Otranto" is that

00:19:27.441 --> 00:19:30.941
no space is beyond the reach of authority.

00:19:33.880 --> 00:19:38.082
And the fact that there's
this constant crossover

00:19:38.082 --> 00:19:40.896
between the Gothic and the real in "Otranto"

00:19:40.896 --> 00:19:45.018
invites us to notice how
both modes involve acts

00:19:45.018 --> 00:19:47.610
of what I'm calling
overreaching authority.

00:19:47.610 --> 00:19:50.110
So where some kind of coercion

00:19:52.165 --> 00:19:56.366
involves a kind of
infiltration into minds,

00:19:56.366 --> 00:19:59.189
into bedrooms, into bodies.

00:19:59.189 --> 00:20:03.106
These things take place
in novels of all kinds.

00:20:04.370 --> 00:20:07.037
Despite the ways in which novels

00:20:09.322 --> 00:20:13.004
stake out a very strong spacial discipline

00:20:13.004 --> 00:20:15.379
or boundary creation for themselves.

00:20:15.379 --> 00:20:16.734
We've talked about doors and things

00:20:16.734 --> 00:20:18.997
but just the big spaces of novels.

00:20:18.997 --> 00:20:22.626
Country houses, the picaresque mode,

00:20:22.626 --> 00:20:24.949
the castle, the grotto, the vault.

00:20:24.949 --> 00:20:28.386
These are all places
where we might think that

00:20:28.386 --> 00:20:30.585
authority might be able to be contained

00:20:30.585 --> 00:20:33.418
rather than continue to overreach.

00:20:34.964 --> 00:20:37.973
But novels are places
where everything can reach

00:20:37.973 --> 00:20:39.723
into everything else.

00:20:41.429 --> 00:20:43.465
Often ludicrously.

00:20:43.465 --> 00:20:46.222
And really the only place
I can think of in "Otranto"

00:20:46.222 --> 00:20:50.701
where overreaching or
reaching is not as successful,

00:20:50.701 --> 00:20:55.023
is not achieved, is when Theodore
can't reopen the trap door

00:20:55.023 --> 00:20:58.690
and reach into the
tunnel or whatever it is.

00:21:00.037 --> 00:21:01.167
So that's point one.

00:21:01.167 --> 00:21:05.344
Point two is that there is a
group of contemporary thinkers

00:21:05.344 --> 00:21:09.880
like people now, who are
interested in revisiting the status

00:21:09.880 --> 00:21:13.899
of faith and belief as a post-Christian

00:21:13.899 --> 00:21:17.066
or new humanist form of understanding.

00:21:17.904 --> 00:21:20.693
These people are trying to
think about the validity

00:21:20.693 --> 00:21:23.672
of convictions of a
certain type of conviction

00:21:23.672 --> 00:21:26.255
that is obligatory, involuntary

00:21:27.314 --> 00:21:31.740
and which is oriented toward
idealization and abstraction

00:21:31.740 --> 00:21:35.865
rather than toward
empiricism or materiality.

00:21:35.865 --> 00:21:40.032
So the key terms here are a
type of understanding that is

00:21:41.497 --> 00:21:44.774
conferred upon the
individual as an obligation

00:21:44.774 --> 00:21:48.062
and which reaches toward an
idealization or an abstraction.

00:21:48.062 --> 00:21:50.501
Takes us out of the empirical.

00:21:50.501 --> 00:21:53.251
So these thinkers are arguing for

00:21:54.882 --> 00:21:59.012
renewed attention actually
to a form of overreaching.

00:21:59.012 --> 00:22:02.735
The reaching of obligation into places

00:22:02.735 --> 00:22:04.566
that have scrupulously been freed

00:22:04.566 --> 00:22:08.376
from the politics of obligation,
like minds and feelings.

00:22:08.376 --> 00:22:10.401
This is supposed to be what
happens in the 18th century.

00:22:10.401 --> 00:22:12.203
Turns out it doesn't work.

00:22:12.203 --> 00:22:14.444
People are revisiting this.

00:22:14.444 --> 00:22:16.921
The question that's
prevailing in a certain strain

00:22:16.921 --> 00:22:19.233
of modern contemporary philosophy

00:22:19.233 --> 00:22:21.656
is whether it's possible
to perform an action

00:22:21.656 --> 00:22:25.823
or hold a position without
choice, but still be free.

00:22:27.215 --> 00:22:30.105
So in other words, there is a return to

00:22:30.105 --> 00:22:33.772
pre-secular ideas about
belief and behavior.

00:22:35.109 --> 00:22:38.526
About the idea that beliefs and behaviors

00:22:40.348 --> 00:22:42.265
can be made obligatory.

00:22:44.184 --> 00:22:48.739
Which in the early modern
period would have been

00:22:48.739 --> 00:22:51.762
constructed by inflexible,
among other things

00:22:51.762 --> 00:22:55.359
by inflexible religious
forms, forms of belief.

00:22:55.359 --> 00:22:58.903
And this type of obligation
has been called obedience.

00:22:58.903 --> 00:23:02.389
And obedience is pretty out of fashion.

00:23:02.389 --> 00:23:06.241
So the theoretical move among this group

00:23:06.241 --> 00:23:09.666
of post-Christian new humanist scholars

00:23:09.666 --> 00:23:12.715
is to re-constitute obedience,

00:23:12.715 --> 00:23:16.901
but to lose the belief
and behavior part of it.

00:23:16.901 --> 00:23:19.818
So to think of a form of obligation

00:23:21.178 --> 00:23:24.917
that might be freeing
because it doesn't produce

00:23:24.917 --> 00:23:28.303
prescriptions around belief or action.

00:23:28.303 --> 00:23:30.720
So it's quite subtle, really.

00:23:32.228 --> 00:23:36.519
So the religious anthropologist Talal Asad

00:23:36.519 --> 00:23:39.648
has summarized what I've just said as

00:23:39.648 --> 00:23:42.940
the notion that formality is necessarily

00:23:42.940 --> 00:23:46.690
an external form of
coercion is questionable.

00:23:48.904 --> 00:23:52.143
In other words, he's asking
whether it's possible

00:23:52.143 --> 00:23:54.252
to enter a system of
meaning without choice

00:23:54.252 --> 00:23:57.985
and yet not be unacceptably
constrained by it.

00:23:57.985 --> 00:23:59.985
Okay, so my third point.

00:24:03.211 --> 00:24:07.968
Which as advertised, is not
precisely related to one or two.

00:24:07.968 --> 00:24:10.702
Is that we think of the 18th century

00:24:10.702 --> 00:24:13.336
as the moment in which critique emerges

00:24:13.336 --> 00:24:15.086
as a way of thinking.

00:24:16.231 --> 00:24:19.398
So quick revision of what critique is.

00:24:21.057 --> 00:24:25.224
It's an attempt to problematize
or trouble obligation.

00:24:26.645 --> 00:24:29.155
Specifically the kind of obligation

00:24:29.155 --> 00:24:32.118
that is involved in
the production of facts

00:24:32.118 --> 00:24:35.205
and other fixed or immovable truths.

00:24:35.205 --> 00:24:38.210
So facts command acquiescence.

00:24:38.210 --> 00:24:40.318
Not only to what they inform us of

00:24:40.318 --> 00:24:42.323
but of how they do the informing.

00:24:42.323 --> 00:24:44.490
We have to submit to that.

00:24:45.509 --> 00:24:48.092
And critique re-installs a care

00:24:49.273 --> 00:24:52.318
for personal interiority and inwardness

00:24:52.318 --> 00:24:56.212
in an effort to override the
rise of the modern state.

00:24:56.212 --> 00:24:58.594
I get that that's a super duper summary

00:24:58.594 --> 00:25:00.349
but let's just go with it for now.

00:25:00.349 --> 00:25:03.964
Critique sort of has a preference for

00:25:03.964 --> 00:25:05.822
what in a different political moment

00:25:05.822 --> 00:25:07.747
we might call the soft truth.

00:25:07.747 --> 00:25:09.420
And what I mean by that

00:25:09.420 --> 00:25:13.528
is knowledge that must be
produced counterintuitively

00:25:13.528 --> 00:25:17.611
that depends upon logic,
that infiltrates the box

00:25:18.638 --> 00:25:20.762
or the trap door of a fact

00:25:20.762 --> 00:25:24.012
and reveals it to be a kind of fiction.

00:25:25.971 --> 00:25:28.388
And you know, a few years ago

00:25:30.429 --> 00:25:33.763
Bruno Latour got very worried about that

00:25:33.763 --> 00:25:37.210
and gave a critique of
critique in which he said that

00:25:37.210 --> 00:25:40.330
it was time to, or maybe it was time to

00:25:40.330 --> 00:25:45.318
restore real facts and to
defend them against the charge

00:25:45.318 --> 00:25:46.401
that they are

00:25:48.613 --> 00:25:51.349
fake facts or liberal conspiracies.

00:25:51.349 --> 00:25:53.249
In other words, that the left was now

00:25:53.249 --> 00:25:56.548
being obligated to do
what it had previously

00:25:56.548 --> 00:25:58.746
had to move so decisively away from.

00:25:58.746 --> 00:26:01.329
Namely to restore the fact as a

00:26:04.109 --> 00:26:06.526
primary epistemological unit.

00:26:08.306 --> 00:26:09.479
Okay.

00:26:09.479 --> 00:26:10.812
So that's three.

00:26:13.049 --> 00:26:14.309
Point four.

00:26:14.309 --> 00:26:19.201
The Walpole that I work
with is the wrong Walpole.

00:26:19.201 --> 00:26:22.701
I work with the unfashionable one, Robert.

00:26:24.185 --> 00:26:27.018
Who obviously was Horace's father.

00:26:29.041 --> 00:26:32.964
And it's interesting to me
that Horace is the third son.

00:26:32.964 --> 00:26:35.552
That seems relevant somehow.

00:26:35.552 --> 00:26:40.407
Anyway, Walpole, Robert
Walpole's administration.

00:26:40.407 --> 00:26:42.223
Again, this is a massive summary.

00:26:42.223 --> 00:26:45.140
But it really was invested in facts

00:26:47.622 --> 00:26:51.525
and it was invested in the
fictional machinery of statehood

00:26:51.525 --> 00:26:54.525
that was required to generate facts.

00:26:56.353 --> 00:27:00.520
Walpole and the Whigs of the
earlier 18th century were

00:27:01.937 --> 00:27:06.852
their commitment to liberty
produced a model of statehood

00:27:06.852 --> 00:27:11.019
in which participation
became entirely involuntary.

00:27:14.018 --> 00:27:17.105
So one of the chief spokespeople

00:27:17.105 --> 00:27:21.268
for Robert Walpole's model of statehood.

00:27:21.268 --> 00:27:25.268
A kind of hygienic, very
efficient secular state

00:27:26.349 --> 00:27:30.099
that was separate from the
church but had a national church

00:27:30.099 --> 00:27:33.267
was a guy called Benjamin Hoadly.

00:27:33.267 --> 00:27:36.141
Who some people might have heard
of, some people won't have.

00:27:36.141 --> 00:27:38.625
He delivered a very contentious sermon

00:27:38.625 --> 00:27:41.875
at a particular moment during Walpole's

00:27:42.936 --> 00:27:45.502
actually it was before
Walpole became Prime Minister

00:27:45.502 --> 00:27:48.819
it was in 1717, but he continued
on into Walpole's period

00:27:48.819 --> 00:27:50.825
and became his sort of propagandist.

00:27:50.825 --> 00:27:54.325
Anyway, Hoadly said that
an act of Parliament

00:27:54.325 --> 00:27:57.341
should not reach to
the inward disposition.

00:27:57.341 --> 00:28:02.031
And he used this claim as
the basis for designing

00:28:02.031 --> 00:28:04.328
a kind of contemporary theology

00:28:04.328 --> 00:28:07.415
that pushed belief, pushed religion,

00:28:07.415 --> 00:28:10.452
pushed abstraction and idealization

00:28:10.452 --> 00:28:13.285
to the margins of the social world

00:28:14.448 --> 00:28:17.687
and in-stated what we think
of as the modern state.

00:28:17.687 --> 00:28:21.699
And what I want to do,
really quickly is read

00:28:21.699 --> 00:28:25.125
a passage from the sermon that Hoadly gave

00:28:25.125 --> 00:28:26.813
in which he laid this out.

00:28:26.813 --> 00:28:29.617
And what I want you to
notice about the passage

00:28:29.617 --> 00:28:31.784
is its Gothic elaboration.

00:28:33.375 --> 00:28:35.474
It's extremely overwrought.

00:28:35.474 --> 00:28:39.898
It relies on a kind of
theological hysteria.

00:28:39.898 --> 00:28:42.002
Though without sound effects.

00:28:42.002 --> 00:28:44.725
And what I want us to think about here

00:28:44.725 --> 00:28:47.642
is this idea that the production of

00:28:51.094 --> 00:28:55.261
an un-Gothic state, of a
state of material realities

00:28:57.794 --> 00:29:00.794
uncontaminated by their overreaching

00:29:02.368 --> 00:29:04.535
into other closed systems.

00:29:06.892 --> 00:29:10.827
That the production of that also produces

00:29:10.827 --> 00:29:12.983
a kind of Gothic register.

00:29:12.983 --> 00:29:14.305
So here's the passage.

00:29:14.305 --> 00:29:15.671
"If any men upon Earth

00:29:15.671 --> 00:29:18.010
"have a right to add to
the sanction of His laws

00:29:18.010 --> 00:29:19.413
"that is to increase the number

00:29:19.413 --> 00:29:22.115
"or alter the nature of
the rewards and punishments

00:29:22.115 --> 00:29:25.115
"of His subjects in matters
of conscience or salvation,

00:29:25.115 --> 00:29:27.425
"they are so far kings in His stead

00:29:27.425 --> 00:29:28.932
"and reign in their own kingdom"

00:29:28.932 --> 00:29:31.646
Him here being Christ "and not in His.

00:29:31.646 --> 00:29:33.707
"Though it is whenever
they erect tribunals

00:29:33.707 --> 00:29:36.310
"and exercise the judgment
over the consciences of men

00:29:36.310 --> 00:29:38.195
"and assume themselves
to the determination

00:29:38.195 --> 00:29:40.193
"of such points as cannot be determined

00:29:40.193 --> 00:29:43.518
"but by one who knows the hearts

00:29:43.518 --> 00:29:45.301
"or when they make any of their own

00:29:45.301 --> 00:29:46.575
"declarations or decisions

00:29:46.575 --> 00:29:49.270
"to concern and affect the
state of Christ's subjects

00:29:49.270 --> 00:29:50.883
"with regard to the favor of God.

00:29:50.883 --> 00:29:54.320
"This is so far the
taking of Christ's kingdom

00:29:54.320 --> 00:29:58.403
"out of his hands and
placing it into their own."

00:29:59.414 --> 00:30:01.986
So I'm going to end by reflecting

00:30:01.986 --> 00:30:04.007
that it took an enormous amount

00:30:04.007 --> 00:30:06.840
rhetorically speaking, cognitively

00:30:08.123 --> 00:30:09.456
and in terms of.

00:30:12.031 --> 00:30:13.736
Well, it took a lot, it took a lot

00:30:13.736 --> 00:30:17.277
to segregate conscience and action.

00:30:17.277 --> 00:30:20.705
To separate the world of the fact

00:30:20.705 --> 00:30:24.872
from the world of the
idealization or abstraction.

00:30:26.710 --> 00:30:29.263
And a lot of that work was done

00:30:29.263 --> 00:30:32.270
during Walpole's period in office.

00:30:32.270 --> 00:30:34.937
And that I think that "Otranto" is

00:30:38.848 --> 00:30:42.098
considering some of the implications of

00:30:45.185 --> 00:30:46.602
that segregation. Yeah.

00:30:48.919 --> 00:30:51.919
(audience clapping)

00:30:58.149 --> 00:31:00.620
- Alright I'm really happy to be here.

00:31:00.620 --> 00:31:02.682
I wanted to thank Jonathan and Nicole

00:31:02.682 --> 00:31:04.399
and everyone else for being here.

00:31:04.399 --> 00:31:07.453
I really enjoyed hearing all the panelists

00:31:07.453 --> 00:31:10.097
and seeing lots of connections between

00:31:10.097 --> 00:31:12.733
the observations we've been making.

00:31:12.733 --> 00:31:14.387
What I'm gonna be talking about today

00:31:14.387 --> 00:31:16.300
relates in a roundabout kind of way

00:31:16.300 --> 00:31:19.271
to the book project I'm
working on at the moment.

00:31:19.271 --> 00:31:22.477
And I wanted to begin by
situating a little bit

00:31:22.477 --> 00:31:25.892
and explaining that
this second book project

00:31:25.892 --> 00:31:30.415
in its current iteration
emerged from a crisis I had

00:31:30.415 --> 00:31:32.614
about two or three months ago

00:31:32.614 --> 00:31:36.957
where I was just sort of
pathologically procrastinating

00:31:36.957 --> 00:31:40.285
and found that I did not
want to write the book

00:31:40.285 --> 00:31:44.000
that I had designed for myself to write.

00:31:44.000 --> 00:31:46.143
Which was, it was titled "Suspended World"

00:31:46.143 --> 00:31:48.771
and it was meant to be about
these kinds of metaphors

00:31:48.771 --> 00:31:51.519
or delicate enclosures, like bubbles

00:31:51.519 --> 00:31:54.195
and other types of spaces

00:31:54.195 --> 00:31:55.540
and thinking about how those worked

00:31:55.540 --> 00:31:57.701
as metaphors for fiction in the period.

00:31:57.701 --> 00:32:00.672
But I'd become really just fascinated by

00:32:00.672 --> 00:32:04.504
Hogarth's account of intricacy
in "The Analysis of Beauty."

00:32:04.504 --> 00:32:05.824
And I kept telling everybody

00:32:05.824 --> 00:32:07.248
I just want to write about intricacy.

00:32:07.248 --> 00:32:10.354
I just want to shelve that whole thing.

00:32:10.354 --> 00:32:13.746
And then this is a very, very
kind of LA epiphany story.

00:32:13.746 --> 00:32:16.645
I was driving along Sunset Boulevard.

00:32:16.645 --> 00:32:18.424
And if you've ever driven along Sunset

00:32:18.424 --> 00:32:20.924
it's an extremely winding road

00:32:22.827 --> 00:32:25.714
full of these sort of hairpin bends.

00:32:25.714 --> 00:32:28.253
And I suddenly decided that

00:32:28.253 --> 00:32:31.631
no, this was a book about
two different figures.

00:32:31.631 --> 00:32:33.190
And so I re-titled it.

00:32:33.190 --> 00:32:36.906
I actually pulled off
Sunset to write this down.

00:32:36.906 --> 00:32:38.480
And I decided it was gonna be called

00:32:38.480 --> 00:32:40.820
"The Thread and the Shell:
Forms of Attachment

00:32:40.820 --> 00:32:42.866
"in 18th-century Literature."

00:32:42.866 --> 00:32:45.487
And that it was gonna be about these two

00:32:45.487 --> 00:32:49.728
paradigms or gestalts, for
the way in which people,

00:32:49.728 --> 00:32:53.848
readers in the 18th century
are being invited to read.

00:32:53.848 --> 00:32:57.454
One of which is a model of
pursuit and following a thread

00:32:57.454 --> 00:33:00.021
and the other one is of entering

00:33:00.021 --> 00:33:04.280
a kind of delicate enclosure,
and that's the shell.

00:33:04.280 --> 00:33:08.447
So in the course of bringing
these two ideas together

00:33:09.938 --> 00:33:13.605
I've become really
fascinated with Daedalus.

00:33:14.793 --> 00:33:17.608
For reasons partly to do with
the nature of his inventions

00:33:17.608 --> 00:33:20.536
and his interest in
both threads and shells.

00:33:20.536 --> 00:33:21.981
So if this paper had a title

00:33:21.981 --> 00:33:25.694
it would be called Daedalian "Otranto."

00:33:25.694 --> 00:33:26.872
Except that I don't really know

00:33:26.872 --> 00:33:29.528
how to pronounce the word, Daedalian.

00:33:29.528 --> 00:33:32.138
Daedalian, so I won't call it that

00:33:32.138 --> 00:33:34.557
but that's what it would
be if it had a title.

00:33:34.557 --> 00:33:35.515
Okay.

00:33:35.515 --> 00:33:37.949
So the adjective Daedalian,

00:33:37.949 --> 00:33:41.934
which I only discovered maybe a month ago

00:33:41.934 --> 00:33:45.533
means of or in the style of Daedalus.

00:33:45.533 --> 00:33:48.008
Skillful, ingenious formed with art

00:33:48.008 --> 00:33:50.691
resembling the Labyrinth of Daedalus

00:33:50.691 --> 00:33:53.989
that he designed at Crete
that housed the Minotaur.

00:33:53.989 --> 00:33:55.363
Intricate and maze-like.

00:33:55.363 --> 00:33:56.955
So specifically my interests in this

00:33:56.955 --> 00:33:59.872
are kind of synonyms for intricate.

00:34:00.958 --> 00:34:03.133
But it's also, and this picks up on

00:34:03.133 --> 00:34:04.759
what Sophie was talking about.

00:34:04.759 --> 00:34:08.926
It's a term that Bruno Latour
uses and turns into a noun.

00:34:09.860 --> 00:34:14.027
So for Latour, a Daedalian
is a technical mediation

00:34:15.267 --> 00:34:17.017
that creates detours.

00:34:18.495 --> 00:34:20.346
And I think this actually also

00:34:20.346 --> 00:34:22.786
kind of connects to what
you were saying about

00:34:22.786 --> 00:34:26.146
the question of whether
something can be constraining

00:34:26.146 --> 00:34:28.968
but you can also be free at the same time.

00:34:28.968 --> 00:34:31.295
So a Daedalian constrained behavior

00:34:31.295 --> 00:34:33.855
but not in a way that's deterministic.

00:34:33.855 --> 00:34:36.914
But that rather sort of pushes
you in certain directions.

00:34:36.914 --> 00:34:39.115
And his main example,
this is in "Pandora's Hope."

00:34:39.115 --> 00:34:42.495
His main example of a
Daedalian is a speed bump.

00:34:42.495 --> 00:34:45.938
But in that same chapter
where he discusses

00:34:45.938 --> 00:34:48.438
the Daedalian, the speed
bumps the Daedalian.

00:34:48.438 --> 00:34:51.762
He also says that fiction is a Daedalian.

00:34:51.762 --> 00:34:55.011
Fictions are also detours that we can make

00:34:55.011 --> 00:34:58.795
as he says, without leaving our seats.

00:34:58.795 --> 00:35:01.459
So with this concept in mind

00:35:01.459 --> 00:35:05.626
I decided that Walpole strikes
me as a Daedalian writer.

00:35:06.648 --> 00:35:09.797
Someone who, like Daedalus himself,

00:35:09.797 --> 00:35:12.218
most famously in his labyrinth

00:35:12.218 --> 00:35:16.540
shapes matter in a way
that creates detours.

00:35:16.540 --> 00:35:19.833
Walpole's letters, and I'm gonna be citing

00:35:19.833 --> 00:35:22.952
bits of text that have already
come up, fascinatingly today.

00:35:22.952 --> 00:35:25.895
Walpole's letters refer to
both reading and writing

00:35:25.895 --> 00:35:30.737
as a matter of imaginatively
moving bodies in space.

00:35:30.737 --> 00:35:34.326
Moving or stilling, I
should say, bodies in space.

00:35:34.326 --> 00:35:38.493
So when he's contemplating
adding a bower to Strawberry Hill.

00:35:39.413 --> 00:35:41.788
He wonders if he, and this is a quotation.

00:35:41.788 --> 00:35:45.629
"Must go and read Spenser and
wade through his allegories

00:35:45.629 --> 00:35:49.212
"and drawling stanzas
to get at a picture." End quote.

00:35:50.084 --> 00:35:52.242
And we kind of have this
sense in that moment

00:35:52.242 --> 00:35:53.903
that he really wants a snapshot.

00:35:53.903 --> 00:35:57.702
He wants a picture of romance, of a bower.

00:35:57.702 --> 00:36:00.079
He doesn't want a peregrination

00:36:00.079 --> 00:36:02.157
or to have to pursue something.

00:36:02.157 --> 00:36:04.457
But Spenser demands pursuit

00:36:04.457 --> 00:36:08.257
and that kind of reading as a detour.

00:36:08.257 --> 00:36:10.257
Then on other occasions,

00:36:11.149 --> 00:36:13.985
the activity of spooling out narrative

00:36:13.985 --> 00:36:17.788
just lurches to a stop very suddenly.

00:36:17.788 --> 00:36:20.755
When he's writing "Otranto," he recalls.

00:36:20.755 --> 00:36:22.582
And this is the part of the passage

00:36:22.582 --> 00:36:24.938
that Cindy quoted earlier.

00:36:24.938 --> 00:36:26.873
"I was so engrossed with my tale

00:36:26.873 --> 00:36:30.143
"that one evening I wrote from
the time I had drunk my tea,

00:36:30.143 --> 00:36:31.146
"about six o'clock

00:36:31.146 --> 00:36:33.308
"til half an hour after one in the morning

00:36:33.308 --> 00:36:35.071
"when my hand and fingers were so weary

00:36:35.071 --> 00:36:37.494
"that I could not hold the
pen to finish the sentence

00:36:37.494 --> 00:36:40.355
"but left Matilda and Isabella talking

00:36:40.355 --> 00:36:42.938
"in the middle of a paragraph."

00:36:44.111 --> 00:36:45.444
So there I think

00:36:47.769 --> 00:36:50.906
or we have both of those
situations in mind.

00:36:50.906 --> 00:36:53.488
The wading through Spenser
to get at a picture

00:36:53.488 --> 00:36:56.406
and the kind of writing in a kind of rush

00:36:56.406 --> 00:37:00.010
and then leaving Matilda and Isabella

00:37:00.010 --> 00:37:01.927
marooned mid paragraph.

00:37:03.028 --> 00:37:06.077
I think Walpole shapes space in such a way

00:37:06.077 --> 00:37:09.508
as to produce these kinds of situations.

00:37:09.508 --> 00:37:12.443
in "Otranto," in the novel itself

00:37:12.443 --> 00:37:15.110
he shapes space in such a way,

00:37:15.110 --> 00:37:17.528
that enclosures turn into passages

00:37:17.528 --> 00:37:21.047
and passages turn into enclosures.

00:37:21.047 --> 00:37:25.214
In which tableaus move and moving
bodies stop in their tracks.

00:37:27.006 --> 00:37:30.006
And I think, in staging these shifts

00:37:30.950 --> 00:37:33.395
between stillness and motion

00:37:33.395 --> 00:37:35.662
he's reflecting on the relationship

00:37:35.662 --> 00:37:38.609
between two modes of representation.

00:37:38.609 --> 00:37:39.494
And this is where I'm getting

00:37:39.494 --> 00:37:41.570
to the kind of thread and shell idea.

00:37:41.570 --> 00:37:45.128
That in which a frame encloses content

00:37:45.128 --> 00:37:48.617
and that in which content
unfolds progressively

00:37:48.617 --> 00:37:52.580
in a way that needs to
be pursued or followed.

00:37:52.580 --> 00:37:54.755
So the famous moment in which

00:37:54.755 --> 00:37:58.493
the portrait of Manfred's
grandfather quits its panel

00:37:58.493 --> 00:38:01.546
is only the most striking
of several instances

00:38:01.546 --> 00:38:04.609
in which a frame becomes a threshold.

00:38:04.609 --> 00:38:07.359
And this is itself as
I've discovered recently

00:38:07.359 --> 00:38:09.647
through reading and lastly about Daedalus,

00:38:09.647 --> 00:38:12.458
a kind of Daedalian spectacle.

00:38:12.458 --> 00:38:14.398
So Daedalian, I should just decide

00:38:14.398 --> 00:38:15.523
how I'm gonna pronounce that word.

00:38:15.523 --> 00:38:18.589
Daedalian sculpture is sculpture that is

00:38:18.589 --> 00:38:22.834
supposedly so lifelike
that Daedalus was said

00:38:22.834 --> 00:38:25.656
to produce statues that
walked, or that moved.

00:38:25.656 --> 00:38:28.323
And Aristophanes has a lost play

00:38:29.504 --> 00:38:32.736
that supposedly turns
on a statue of Daedalus'

00:38:32.736 --> 00:38:34.903
that just walks and moves.

00:38:36.705 --> 00:38:40.141
The moment in which the
portrait quits its panel

00:38:40.141 --> 00:38:43.196
foreshadows one that
follows in quick succession.

00:38:43.196 --> 00:38:45.833
Another one that's been
alluded to several times today

00:38:45.833 --> 00:38:47.592
in which Theodore's supposed to be

00:38:47.592 --> 00:38:49.586
confined under the fatal helmet,

00:38:49.586 --> 00:38:53.671
escapes from the helmet
into the vault below.

00:38:53.671 --> 00:38:56.063
When Manfred finds him, he discovers.

00:38:56.063 --> 00:38:58.328
And this is another quotation that

00:38:58.328 --> 00:39:00.661
"one of the cheeks of the enchanted cask

00:39:00.661 --> 00:39:03.974
"had forced its way through
the pavement of the court

00:39:03.974 --> 00:39:07.298
"as his servants had let
it fall over the peasant.

00:39:07.298 --> 00:39:10.426
"And had broken through into
the vault, leaving a gap

00:39:10.426 --> 00:39:14.674
"through which the peasant
had pressed himself."

00:39:14.674 --> 00:39:16.229
And I'm gonna come back a little bit

00:39:16.229 --> 00:39:18.230
to that language just in a minute.

00:39:18.230 --> 00:39:19.628
But just to say, in "Otranto"

00:39:19.628 --> 00:39:23.152
every time providence
opens a gap in the roof

00:39:23.152 --> 00:39:25.735
it also slams shut a trap door.

00:39:26.690 --> 00:39:28.475
(audience laughs)

00:39:28.475 --> 00:39:32.726
Isabel urges Theodore to shut
the trap door after he exits.

00:39:32.726 --> 00:39:34.883
But as we've just heard, it slams shut

00:39:34.883 --> 00:39:37.697
before he has the chance,
thereby as John was saying

00:39:37.697 --> 00:39:41.021
signaling his presence to Manfred.

00:39:41.021 --> 00:39:42.708
"'What noise was that,' said Manfred

00:39:42.708 --> 00:39:44.574
"'Which I heard as I entered the cloister?'

00:39:44.574 --> 00:39:46.054
"'A door clapped,' said the peasant.

00:39:46.054 --> 00:39:48.409
"'I heard it as well as you.'"

00:39:48.409 --> 00:39:51.407
Moments like these turn
passages into enclosures

00:39:51.407 --> 00:39:53.552
and enclosures into passages.

00:39:53.552 --> 00:39:57.397
In ways, and I guess this
is my gambit or claim here.

00:39:57.397 --> 00:40:01.955
That seem to assert the
agency of the frame itself.

00:40:01.955 --> 00:40:04.147
It is the helmet, not Theodore

00:40:04.147 --> 00:40:06.361
that forces its way through the floor

00:40:06.361 --> 00:40:08.455
in that passage I read before,

00:40:08.455 --> 00:40:13.390
with the cheeks breaking
down into the floor below.

00:40:13.390 --> 00:40:16.679
It's the door that claps,
not Theodore who shuts it.

00:40:16.679 --> 00:40:18.779
And I wonder if we might see these moments

00:40:18.779 --> 00:40:20.571
and this is speculative.

00:40:20.571 --> 00:40:24.288
As ones in which the border
exerts its own agency

00:40:24.288 --> 00:40:28.204
as textual incarnations of the arabesque.

00:40:28.204 --> 00:40:31.982
That movement in which
decorative and ornamental forms

00:40:31.982 --> 00:40:34.899
associated with borders and frames.

00:40:36.899 --> 00:40:40.737
That movement associated
with those kinds of frames

00:40:40.737 --> 00:40:44.904
and also often comprising
of leaf work and shell work.

00:40:47.261 --> 00:40:50.127
So then my final, this is my final point.

00:40:50.127 --> 00:40:52.577
But I'm gonna try to
extemporise a little bit

00:40:52.577 --> 00:40:54.744
in explaining what I mean.

00:40:55.598 --> 00:40:59.108
I think that "Otranto's"
merging of frame and unframed

00:40:59.108 --> 00:41:02.165
passage and enclosure
draws our attention then

00:41:02.165 --> 00:41:05.802
to these two different
metaphors I want to suggest.

00:41:05.802 --> 00:41:09.035
By which we understand representations.

00:41:09.035 --> 00:41:11.861
On the one hand, it's a pursuit.

00:41:11.861 --> 00:41:14.550
Manfred, and this is another quotation.

00:41:14.550 --> 00:41:17.550
Is "Impatient to unravel this clue."

00:41:19.597 --> 00:41:22.314
And again this is something
I didn't know until recently.

00:41:22.314 --> 00:41:23.977
But that probably many of you know

00:41:23.977 --> 00:41:26.768
that the word clue comes from

00:41:26.768 --> 00:41:30.518
the ball of thread that you
use to navigate a labyrinth.

00:41:30.518 --> 00:41:34.421
And so Walpole is using
that quite literally there.

00:41:34.421 --> 00:41:36.921
The idea of unraveling a clue.

00:41:39.439 --> 00:41:41.195
But at the same time,

00:41:41.195 --> 00:41:43.277
and I think Gena was talking about this.

00:41:43.277 --> 00:41:45.820
He's also transfixed by the helmet

00:41:45.820 --> 00:41:48.804
and that's like another
model of kind of suspense

00:41:48.804 --> 00:41:50.554
and being transfixed.

00:41:52.690 --> 00:41:54.403
And you know, I think likewise in the way

00:41:54.403 --> 00:41:56.486
"Otranto" works as a novel.

00:41:57.612 --> 00:42:00.534
It's clearly, and Walpole
alludes to this in his preface.

00:42:00.534 --> 00:42:02.859
It's trying to kind of
produce this kind of suspense

00:42:02.859 --> 00:42:04.853
and tension in the reader

00:42:04.853 --> 00:42:08.819
that we might associate more
with this pursuit model.

00:42:08.819 --> 00:42:12.999
But it's also, and I
think it was again Cindy

00:42:12.999 --> 00:42:15.379
who reminded me of this.

00:42:15.379 --> 00:42:18.605
He characterizes the way
he comes up with a novel

00:42:18.605 --> 00:42:21.355
as deriving from, or he says that

00:42:22.835 --> 00:42:24.988
his head is filled with Gothic story.

00:42:24.988 --> 00:42:27.541
That it's like, the we're
kind of traveling into that

00:42:27.541 --> 00:42:30.311
space of the head filled with Gothic story

00:42:30.311 --> 00:42:32.640
in a way that sort of suggests more of a

00:42:32.640 --> 00:42:36.807
sort of static image that we
can enter a living picture.

00:42:37.697 --> 00:42:39.030
So I think then,

00:42:41.903 --> 00:42:43.934
I think that there's way in which

00:42:43.934 --> 00:42:47.243
the novel is drawing our attention

00:42:47.243 --> 00:42:50.267
to what we might call kind
of shellwork on the one hand

00:42:50.267 --> 00:42:51.760
and threadwork in the other.

00:42:51.760 --> 00:42:55.177
In ways in which these are two paradigms.

00:42:56.157 --> 00:42:58.324
But in a way that's really

00:42:59.963 --> 00:43:02.120
I don't know exactly how to describe it.

00:43:02.120 --> 00:43:04.055
But just like vexing.

00:43:04.055 --> 00:43:08.222
And I'll just end by saying
that the word unravel is,

00:43:11.386 --> 00:43:15.729
I've become completely
obsessed with the word unravel.

00:43:15.729 --> 00:43:17.960
An unraveled mystery is both

00:43:17.960 --> 00:43:21.376
a solved mystery and an unsolved one

00:43:21.376 --> 00:43:23.120
because the word unravel means

00:43:23.120 --> 00:43:26.164
both the opposite of the verb ravel

00:43:26.164 --> 00:43:28.664
and the opposite, and unravel.

00:43:31.402 --> 00:43:32.492
I'm gonna get it wrong.

00:43:32.492 --> 00:43:34.562
It just makes my head hurt
so much to think about it.

00:43:34.562 --> 00:43:37.866
But in essence, it's a solved mystery

00:43:37.866 --> 00:43:39.452
and an unsolved mystery.

00:43:39.452 --> 00:43:42.165
And I think that what
Walpole is doing here

00:43:42.165 --> 00:43:44.873
is something like sort of confronting us

00:43:44.873 --> 00:43:47.706
with the limits of these different

00:43:51.598 --> 00:43:54.276
hermeneutic modes or
modes of interpretation

00:43:54.276 --> 00:43:57.358
and kind of just letting
us butt up against them.

00:43:57.358 --> 00:44:01.525
And making our brain hurt by
having them constantly shift.

00:44:02.498 --> 00:44:05.331
Shift modes and shift into another

00:44:06.584 --> 00:44:09.417
just, all the time and constantly.

00:44:10.267 --> 00:44:12.475
So I will leave it there.

00:44:12.475 --> 00:44:13.308
Thank you.

00:44:13.308 --> 00:44:16.308
(audience clapping)

00:44:20.521 --> 00:44:21.609
- Okay.

00:44:21.609 --> 00:44:23.424
Thank you, thank you Jonathan.

00:44:23.424 --> 00:44:25.591
Thank you Nicole and Yale.

00:44:26.458 --> 00:44:29.791
And I feel like the standard here for me

00:44:30.667 --> 00:44:31.882
is the substitute teacher

00:44:31.882 --> 00:44:33.979
because I'm a fill-in for Michael Gamer.

00:44:33.979 --> 00:44:35.989
Okay, so just bear that in mind.

00:44:35.989 --> 00:44:39.444
So I don't write about Walpole or "Otranto."

00:44:39.444 --> 00:44:41.019
And I'll just do the best I can.

00:44:41.019 --> 00:44:42.754
And I was saying to Gena.

00:44:42.754 --> 00:44:46.482
I feel like this is the
gay version of her talk.

00:44:46.482 --> 00:44:48.320
So keep that in mind.

00:44:48.320 --> 00:44:49.360
Okay, so.

00:44:49.360 --> 00:44:52.027
Re-reading "The Castle of Otranto"

00:44:53.103 --> 00:44:54.936
I felt like most text,

00:44:55.811 --> 00:44:59.978
this is one that is probably
best experienced as drag show.

00:45:01.524 --> 00:45:05.313
And I mean like, all
queens kind of drag show.

00:45:05.313 --> 00:45:07.477
And I'm talking about something that

00:45:07.477 --> 00:45:10.122
I guess for Walpolians is so obvious

00:45:10.122 --> 00:45:12.420
that you maybe didn't want
to talk about it today,

00:45:12.420 --> 00:45:13.587
which is camp.

00:45:14.762 --> 00:45:18.345
That sort of special
Walpolian sensibility.

00:45:20.318 --> 00:45:24.068
And I thought for this
group, including Sarah

00:45:25.373 --> 00:45:28.860
but I think also Sean
and Jessie and others.

00:45:28.860 --> 00:45:33.027
We could apply camp as a test
for the experience of wonder.

00:45:34.601 --> 00:45:36.742
And so my question would be

00:45:36.742 --> 00:45:40.909
whether camp and campy pleasure
is antithetical to wonder.

00:45:42.832 --> 00:45:46.999
Whether camp is too knowing,
too surface, too made.

00:45:48.074 --> 00:45:50.574
Now, I think Sarah has already

00:45:52.718 --> 00:45:54.813
provided an answer to this in her book

00:45:54.813 --> 00:45:58.980
which is to say, no it's
not antithetical in novels.

00:46:01.134 --> 00:46:03.582
She makes this gorgeous distinction

00:46:03.582 --> 00:46:06.897
to wonder at and to
wonder about an object.

00:46:06.897 --> 00:46:09.931
So in novel reading, you
have this double experience

00:46:09.931 --> 00:46:13.010
of recognition and estrangement,

00:46:13.010 --> 00:46:17.177
enchantment and disenchantment,
suspense and admiration.

00:46:18.743 --> 00:46:23.495
What she beautifully calls,
the verisimilar marvelous.

00:46:23.495 --> 00:46:25.908
So here's an example of that, I think.

00:46:25.908 --> 00:46:30.085
In "The Castle of Otranto,"
read by Joseph Roach.

00:46:30.085 --> 00:46:31.352
Take it away Joe.

00:46:31.352 --> 00:46:34.339
Oh, okay in this scene also, sorry.

00:46:34.339 --> 00:46:35.604
- Not here.

00:46:35.604 --> 00:46:37.766
- Okay, let me queue you up.

00:46:37.766 --> 00:46:40.516
So of course, Theodore, his shirt

00:46:42.172 --> 00:46:46.141
he's about to be beheaded
or whatever by Manfred

00:46:46.141 --> 00:46:49.819
and it's kind of like in a tennis match

00:46:49.819 --> 00:46:52.380
when Rafael Nadal is playing

00:46:52.380 --> 00:46:54.971
and after the match he
changes his sweaty shirt

00:46:54.971 --> 00:46:59.080
and the whole audience
gasps at his abdominals.

00:46:59.080 --> 00:47:00.489
So it's that kind of moment.

00:47:00.489 --> 00:47:04.626
His shirt falls and reveals the shoulder,

00:47:04.626 --> 00:47:06.384
and reveals the bloody arrow.

00:47:06.384 --> 00:47:08.087
Take it away Professor Roach.

00:47:08.087 --> 00:47:09.643
- "'Gracious heaven!'

00:47:09.643 --> 00:47:11.842
(audience laughs)

00:47:11.842 --> 00:47:13.981
"cried the holy man starting.

00:47:13.981 --> 00:47:15.231
"'What do I see!

00:47:16.185 --> 00:47:18.602
"'Is it my child, my Theodore!'

00:47:19.732 --> 00:47:22.255
"The passions that
ensued must be conceived;

00:47:22.255 --> 00:47:24.107
"They cannot be painted.

00:47:24.107 --> 00:47:27.560
"The tears of the assistants
were suspended by wonder

00:47:27.560 --> 00:47:29.930
"rather than stopped by joy.

00:47:29.930 --> 00:47:32.967
"They seemed to inquire
in the eyes of their lord

00:47:32.967 --> 00:47:34.828
"what they ought to feel.

00:47:34.828 --> 00:47:38.220
"Surprise, doubt, tenderness, respect

00:47:38.220 --> 00:47:42.010
"succeeded each other in
the countenance of the youth.

00:47:42.010 --> 00:47:43.956
"He received, with modest submission,

00:47:43.956 --> 00:47:46.509
"the effusion of the old man's tears,

00:47:46.509 --> 00:47:50.573
"and embraces of giving a loose to hope

00:47:50.573 --> 00:47:54.283
"and suspecting from what had passed,

00:47:54.283 --> 00:47:56.857
"the inflexibility of Manfred's temper,

00:47:56.857 --> 00:48:00.677
"he cast a glance towards
the prince as if to say,

00:48:00.677 --> 00:48:04.677
"Canst thou be unmoved
at such a scene as this?"

00:48:05.729 --> 00:48:07.703
(audience laughs)

00:48:07.703 --> 00:48:10.703
(audience clapping)

00:48:12.227 --> 00:48:14.981
- Okay, so you see the
two experiences there.

00:48:14.981 --> 00:48:17.648
First that suspension of wonder.

00:48:18.687 --> 00:48:22.305
So itself is a kind of
suspension of feeling

00:48:22.305 --> 00:48:24.805
and then the servants have to say.

00:48:24.805 --> 00:48:26.444
Okay, what am I supposed to feel here?

00:48:26.444 --> 00:48:27.505
Am I supposed to feel?

00:48:27.505 --> 00:48:30.714
They're looking to see where to,

00:48:30.714 --> 00:48:32.527
what to slot into that emptiness.

00:48:32.527 --> 00:48:34.982
And then that great moment
where Theodore is like.

00:48:34.982 --> 00:48:39.981
Are you taking this in, are
you seeing how moving this is?

00:48:39.981 --> 00:48:42.314
Okay, so to go back to camp.

00:48:43.308 --> 00:48:47.117
So in the Sontag essay, there
are a few definitions of camp.

00:48:47.117 --> 00:48:49.200
Camp is a logic of taste.

00:48:50.704 --> 00:48:52.537
It's a way of looking.

00:48:53.412 --> 00:48:56.079
It's self-parody plus self-love.

00:48:57.846 --> 00:48:59.679
It's a tender feeling.

00:49:00.893 --> 00:49:05.543
And here's Joseph Roach
last year, or a year before

00:49:05.543 --> 00:49:08.908
at ASECS on Horace Walpole on camp.

00:49:08.908 --> 00:49:10.908
So in Roach's statement.

00:49:12.125 --> 00:49:15.895
"If kitsch, in Milan Kundera's formulation

00:49:15.895 --> 00:49:18.688
"is the complete absence of shit.

00:49:18.688 --> 00:49:21.640
"Think Cinderella's
Castle at Magic Mountain.

00:49:21.640 --> 00:49:26.384
"Then camp by contrast means
kitsch with all the shit

00:49:26.384 --> 00:49:29.199
"self-consciously and artfully restored."

00:49:29.199 --> 00:49:30.553
Okay.

00:49:30.553 --> 00:49:33.136
So number 56 in Sontag's essay.

00:49:34.434 --> 00:49:38.601
"Camp taste is a kind of
love, love for human nature."

00:49:39.616 --> 00:49:42.973
In other words, it doesn't judge.

00:49:42.973 --> 00:49:46.390
So is "The Castle of Otranto," is the novel

00:49:49.281 --> 00:49:53.448
a vehicle to accommodate
and exonerate male feeling?

00:49:54.874 --> 00:49:59.041
Especially as, and Joe's
reading fleshes this out.

00:50:01.608 --> 00:50:05.358
Especially primary
ambivalence towards women.

00:50:07.170 --> 00:50:11.725
In other words, the Harvey
Weinstein story every time.

00:50:11.725 --> 00:50:15.203
That is to say, men's
constitutional incapacity

00:50:15.203 --> 00:50:19.965
to regulate their passions and
women's collective endurance

00:50:19.965 --> 00:50:22.798
of their inevitable victimization.

00:50:25.854 --> 00:50:30.021
Roach writes that camp isn't
about escape, but relief.

00:50:31.762 --> 00:50:35.345
That it isn't transgression,
but expansion.

00:50:36.693 --> 00:50:39.169
And here are some great formulations

00:50:39.169 --> 00:50:42.817
that camp is an earnest insincerity.

00:50:42.817 --> 00:50:46.984
That it's an exclamation point
disguised by a question mark.

00:50:48.183 --> 00:50:51.600
"Camp reveals no hidden depth" he writes.

00:50:52.457 --> 00:50:55.222
"Because what is hiding in plain sight

00:50:55.222 --> 00:50:59.389
"needs all the therapeutic
recuperation it can get."

00:51:00.816 --> 00:51:03.816
So re-reading it now in this moment.

00:51:04.882 --> 00:51:08.625
I thought well what camp knows is that

00:51:08.625 --> 00:51:12.830
the story of heterosexuality
is a really sad one.

00:51:12.830 --> 00:51:15.025
And as Sandra McPherson, in person

00:51:15.025 --> 00:51:18.550
and in her scrupulous
scholarship reminds me.

00:51:18.550 --> 00:51:21.028
It's a story in which men are confused

00:51:21.028 --> 00:51:25.418
and women get hurt or
destroyed all the time.

00:51:25.418 --> 00:51:29.585
Camp allows us to see that
story for everything it's worth.

00:51:30.740 --> 00:51:33.325
You get to feel sorry for it,

00:51:33.325 --> 00:51:36.903
the whole catastrophe
we're tending towards

00:51:36.903 --> 00:51:38.606
in Walpole's language.

00:51:38.606 --> 00:51:42.035
You get to feel sorry for
it, feel sorry for ourselves,

00:51:42.035 --> 00:51:44.706
our loved ones and our enemies.

00:51:44.706 --> 00:51:46.706
But also like we get it.

00:51:49.619 --> 00:51:51.417
Here is Roach again.

00:51:51.417 --> 00:51:55.584
"Camp stages what is too
important to be taken seriously."

00:51:56.792 --> 00:51:59.959
And I would adjust too what is too true

00:52:01.086 --> 00:52:03.404
to be taken seriously.

00:52:03.404 --> 00:52:06.486
Here's where I think that camp may be

00:52:06.486 --> 00:52:08.569
incompatible with wonder.

00:52:09.654 --> 00:52:13.821
And I rely here on Spinoza's
description of wonder.

00:52:14.759 --> 00:52:17.530
Which is, when you behold an object

00:52:17.530 --> 00:52:22.213
that for you has no precedence
in quality or degree.

00:52:22.213 --> 00:52:25.039
And so it absorbs all of your attention

00:52:25.039 --> 00:52:28.866
because you have no chain of
associations that it launches.

00:52:28.866 --> 00:52:33.367
No field of reference in
beholding that object.

00:52:33.367 --> 00:52:34.852
And this is Spinoza.

00:52:34.852 --> 00:52:37.844
"Wonder is the conception of anything

00:52:37.844 --> 00:52:40.658
"wherein the mind comes to a stand

00:52:40.658 --> 00:52:43.469
"because the particular
concept in question

00:52:43.469 --> 00:52:47.189
"has no connection with other concepts."

00:52:47.189 --> 00:52:51.356
In "Otranto" and in camp, we see
the aggressively unavoidable

00:52:53.262 --> 00:52:55.401
precedence of the story.

00:52:55.401 --> 00:52:59.234
Its dogged, tragic and
hilarious persistence.

00:53:00.828 --> 00:53:04.707
So Harvey Weinstein or
Hamlet, or Henry the 8th

00:53:04.707 --> 00:53:07.605
but also thank God, the saucy herald,

00:53:07.605 --> 00:53:10.528
the unhappy princesses, the unknown mutes,

00:53:10.528 --> 00:53:12.165
the handsome cavalier.

00:53:12.165 --> 00:53:14.300
The buried and mangled and bleeding

00:53:14.300 --> 00:53:16.203
and disfigured and dashed to pieces,

00:53:16.203 --> 00:53:19.536
already puny, unathletic body of Conrad.

00:53:21.932 --> 00:53:26.015
My hunch is that camp is
a way to avoid contempt.

00:53:28.043 --> 00:53:32.148
And this is for Spinoza, the
opposite feeling of wonder.

00:53:32.148 --> 00:53:35.420
Contempt is the conception of anything

00:53:35.420 --> 00:53:38.328
which touches the mind so little

00:53:38.328 --> 00:53:40.829
that its presence leads the mind

00:53:40.829 --> 00:53:44.415
to imagine those qualities
which are not in it

00:53:44.415 --> 00:53:47.176
rather than such as are in it.

00:53:47.176 --> 00:53:50.312
An example of contempt here.

00:53:50.312 --> 00:53:52.858
Like last week I went to

00:53:52.858 --> 00:53:56.488
a very straight, very
white millennial wedding.

00:53:56.488 --> 00:54:00.309
And it's a soul destroying experience.

00:54:00.309 --> 00:54:01.853
It's like, you think.

00:54:01.853 --> 00:54:04.100
Is he really gonna kiss the bride?

00:54:04.100 --> 00:54:06.054
Are they really gonna
feed each other cake?

00:54:06.054 --> 00:54:07.924
Is there really gonna be a first dance?

00:54:07.924 --> 00:54:10.241
And they do, they do all of it.

00:54:10.241 --> 00:54:15.106
And it would be horrible
except that a few days before

00:54:15.106 --> 00:54:19.273
I had gone at NYU to the
Halloween Drag Queen Cinderella

00:54:21.778 --> 00:54:23.605
big extravagant performance.

00:54:23.605 --> 00:54:25.108
And there's some way in which

00:54:25.108 --> 00:54:28.775
drag redeems the rituals
of heterosexuality.

00:54:31.175 --> 00:54:35.342
So in camp, you get to eat
the cake you're stuck with.

00:54:36.704 --> 00:54:38.319
No matter that it's made of shit

00:54:38.319 --> 00:54:41.402
and guaranteed to make you sick
and will probably kill you.

00:54:41.402 --> 00:54:44.364
But here's a better
version of that metaphor.

00:54:44.364 --> 00:54:47.794
You don't have to ingest
the cake yourself.

00:54:47.794 --> 00:54:51.167
But you do get to cheer on all the queens

00:54:51.167 --> 00:54:52.414
while they're eating it.

00:54:52.414 --> 00:54:53.702
And not just eating it,

00:54:53.702 --> 00:54:56.683
but ripping off each
other's tiaras over it.

00:54:56.683 --> 00:55:00.456
Stuffing it in each other's
bra cups and jock straps.

00:55:00.456 --> 00:55:02.623
Wrapping the goopy pieces of it

00:55:02.623 --> 00:55:05.631
in glittered wax paper
and tinselated ribbon.

00:55:05.631 --> 00:55:08.590
And then firing all of those packages

00:55:08.590 --> 00:55:11.563
out of a unicorn rainbow
poo shooting cannon

00:55:11.563 --> 00:55:14.426
so that it pours over the whole fiesta

00:55:14.426 --> 00:55:17.254
like confetti at a Donna Summer concert.

00:55:17.254 --> 00:55:20.378
That was the gay version of Gena's thing.

00:55:20.378 --> 00:55:22.148
That's all, that's it.

00:55:22.148 --> 00:55:25.148
(audience clapping)

00:55:30.160 --> 00:55:31.799
- I find that very hard to follow.

00:55:31.799 --> 00:55:33.173
(everyone laughing)

00:55:33.173 --> 00:55:34.971
- That's substitute teacher.

00:55:34.971 --> 00:55:37.865
(everyone laughs)

00:55:37.865 --> 00:55:41.227
- Thanks Jonathan and
Nicole for today, such fun.

00:55:41.227 --> 00:55:45.177
I too am gonna talk about
the sounds of "Otranto."

00:55:45.177 --> 00:55:47.081
So as John beautifully showed us,

00:55:47.081 --> 00:55:50.913
there's lots to say about the
novels and articulate sounds.

00:55:50.913 --> 00:55:54.003
Which anticipate a now
familiar Gothic soundscape

00:55:54.003 --> 00:55:56.545
of the disembodied indefinite eerie.

00:55:56.545 --> 00:55:59.266
For example, a passage John cited.

00:55:59.266 --> 00:56:00.575
"An awful silence reigned

00:56:00.575 --> 00:56:02.556
"throughout those subterraneous regions.

00:56:02.556 --> 00:56:04.404
"Except now and then some blasts of wind

00:56:04.404 --> 00:56:06.527
"that shook the doors [Isabella] had passed

00:56:06.527 --> 00:56:08.287
"and which, graying on the rusty hinges

00:56:08.287 --> 00:56:11.141
"were re-echoed through that
long labyrinth of darkness.

00:56:11.141 --> 00:56:14.177
"Every murmur struck
her with a new terror."

00:56:14.177 --> 00:56:16.016
Isabella van Elferen's book,

00:56:16.016 --> 00:56:20.058
"Gothic Music: The Sounds of
the Uncanny" cites this passage

00:56:20.058 --> 00:56:22.171
as it summarized the Gothic conventions

00:56:22.171 --> 00:56:23.843
that "Otranto" helped shape.

00:56:23.843 --> 00:56:26.243
"The advent of terror
is generally preceded by

00:56:26.243 --> 00:56:28.969
"an unworldly silence aided by

00:56:28.969 --> 00:56:31.470
"meteorological wind, thunder, rain

00:56:31.470 --> 00:56:35.843
"or technical rattling fences,
hissing pipes, circumstances

00:56:35.843 --> 00:56:38.643
"and announced by sounds
that suggest presence.

00:56:38.643 --> 00:56:41.817
"Creaks, echoes, voices, music."

00:56:41.817 --> 00:56:45.167
Indeed, van Elferen treats
echo as the perfect figure

00:56:45.167 --> 00:56:49.448
for what she calls the in/
materiality of Gothic sound.

00:56:49.448 --> 00:56:52.001
An echo "suggests physical presence

00:56:52.001 --> 00:56:53.559
"but it is disconnected from it

00:56:53.559 --> 00:56:56.903
"like a phantom doppelganger
of its former self."

00:56:56.903 --> 00:56:59.620
My new project is an intellectual history

00:56:59.620 --> 00:57:02.809
of the sound of words and of
poetic sound in particular.

00:57:02.809 --> 00:57:05.766
So I want to talk today in
a rather speculative way

00:57:05.766 --> 00:57:08.078
about the novel's articulate sounds.

00:57:08.078 --> 00:57:10.520
The way the sounds link with semantics.

00:57:10.520 --> 00:57:12.424
If Walpole's re-echoing participates

00:57:12.424 --> 00:57:14.807
in the Gothic tradition
of disembodied sounds

00:57:14.807 --> 00:57:16.157
that tantalizingly suggest

00:57:16.157 --> 00:57:18.622
but refuse to confirm some other presence.

00:57:18.622 --> 00:57:21.003
His various ways of
highlighting articulate sounds

00:57:21.003 --> 00:57:22.895
might suggest a different understanding.

00:57:22.895 --> 00:57:25.827
As themselves present and embodied.

00:57:25.827 --> 00:57:29.668
Fascinating work by actually
many people in this room

00:57:29.668 --> 00:57:32.375
has underscored the importance
of the novel's things.

00:57:32.375 --> 00:57:35.627
I'm asking if the novel's
sketches too the possibility

00:57:35.627 --> 00:57:38.643
that words are a kind of thing.

00:57:38.643 --> 00:57:41.948
So approached as many 18th
century writers approached them

00:57:41.948 --> 00:57:44.940
echoes were a useful figure
for this understanding too.

00:57:44.940 --> 00:57:47.190
I have to admit that I've
been thinking way too much

00:57:47.190 --> 00:57:49.297
recently about Pope's "The sound must seem

00:57:49.297 --> 00:57:52.830
"an echo to the sense" and
then echoes in particular.

00:57:52.830 --> 00:57:55.483
Walpole did some thinking
about echoes too.

00:57:55.483 --> 00:57:58.377
Post "Otranto," he wrote a silly
one act performance piece

00:57:58.377 --> 00:58:01.117
featuring the early modern
tradition of Echo Verse.

00:58:01.117 --> 00:58:04.274
In this tradition, words are
compounded material things

00:58:04.274 --> 00:58:05.906
moving through space and time.

00:58:05.906 --> 00:58:07.553
Words bounce around, disaggregate

00:58:07.553 --> 00:58:09.354
and their parts change meaning.

00:58:09.354 --> 00:58:12.616
So in a traditional echo
verse, a speaker asks,

00:58:12.616 --> 00:58:14.958
"Tell me what is that supreme delight?"

00:58:14.958 --> 00:58:17.207
and the echo answers, "Light."

00:58:17.207 --> 00:58:19.353
So delight, light comes apart.

00:58:19.353 --> 00:58:22.875
In Walpole's version, a
hilarious version actually.

00:58:22.875 --> 00:58:26.194
A fairy punishes a man with a
loss of some part of himself.

00:58:26.194 --> 00:58:28.421
But he gets to choose which part.

00:58:28.421 --> 00:58:30.703
He must communicate his choice to echo.

00:58:30.703 --> 00:58:34.114
The man chooses an
expendable part, his tears.

00:58:34.114 --> 00:58:36.884
But the echo catches only ears.

00:58:36.884 --> 00:58:39.547
"I did not say my ears," the man insists.

00:58:39.547 --> 00:58:42.765
But the echo hears only
confirmation, ears.

00:58:42.765 --> 00:58:45.554
Desperate, the man tries to
break the word into parts.

00:58:45.554 --> 00:58:48.513
My T E A R S, but of course

00:58:48.513 --> 00:58:51.013
the echo catches only E A R S.

00:58:52.200 --> 00:58:55.626
Like other Echo Verse, Walpole's
jeu d'esprit treats sounds

00:58:55.626 --> 00:58:58.742
as the building blocks of sense
in a resolutely material way

00:58:58.742 --> 00:59:01.148
combining, recombining, drifting, moving.

00:59:01.148 --> 00:59:03.071
Echoing sounds need not be thought of as

00:59:03.071 --> 00:59:05.842
immaterial phantom
doppelgangers of lost presence.

00:59:05.842 --> 00:59:08.092
They are themselves
present nuggets of meaning

00:59:08.092 --> 00:59:10.315
moving through space and time.

00:59:10.315 --> 00:59:14.505
Walpole's novel seems literally
to begin with something like

00:59:14.505 --> 00:59:18.231
the Echo Verse disaggregation of E A R S.

00:59:18.231 --> 00:59:21.620
For the title page gives the
real Italian writer's name

00:59:21.620 --> 00:59:23.949
as Unophrio Muralto.

00:59:23.949 --> 00:59:25.682
As Sir Walter Scott has suggested

00:59:25.682 --> 00:59:28.782
this name is a sort of
anagram of Horatio Walpole.

00:59:28.782 --> 00:59:31.566
Four letters are missing
and some new ones are added.

00:59:31.566 --> 00:59:34.247
Scott also calls that
a kind of translation.

00:59:34.247 --> 00:59:37.422
And after him, some fantastic
theories have been floated.

00:59:37.422 --> 00:59:41.260
Wal goes French, Mur, apole can be alto,

00:59:41.260 --> 00:59:44.160
Italian for high and so on.

00:59:44.160 --> 00:59:46.531
Letters and sounds pull
apart, drift, recombine.

00:59:46.531 --> 00:59:49.222
They signify and obscure.

00:59:49.222 --> 00:59:50.898
"Otranto" features other sound play

00:59:50.898 --> 00:59:52.962
in the spirit of Echo Verse as well.

00:59:52.962 --> 00:59:54.776
When Matilda faints after overhearing

00:59:54.776 --> 00:59:57.437
Manfred sentence Theodore to death.

00:59:57.437 --> 01:00:01.020
Bianca shrieks, "Help,
help the princess is dead."

01:00:01.020 --> 01:00:02.583
Bianca's words move.

01:00:02.583 --> 01:00:04.814
Manfred hears them and
stops what he is doing

01:00:04.814 --> 01:00:06.699
to figure out what happened.

01:00:06.699 --> 01:00:09.865
We later find out that the
words traveled yet further.

01:00:09.865 --> 01:00:11.980
And as they moved, their meaning shifted.

01:00:11.980 --> 01:00:14.005
A monk visiting the
castle, heard the scream

01:00:14.005 --> 01:00:15.815
and then brought word to the convent

01:00:15.815 --> 01:00:18.601
that another princess, Hippolita was dead.

01:00:18.601 --> 01:00:19.864
Sounds are wayward things.

01:00:19.864 --> 01:00:22.781
They carry meaning but
not always reliably.

01:00:22.781 --> 01:00:25.760
If here, the sounds retain
their proper shapes as words

01:00:25.760 --> 01:00:27.357
unlike tears, ears.

01:00:27.357 --> 01:00:29.081
Walpole also allowed sound shapes

01:00:29.081 --> 01:00:31.129
to morph in their movements.

01:00:31.129 --> 01:00:33.114
Thus his comic malapropism.

01:00:33.114 --> 01:00:36.951
When the servant Jaquez, who
Manfred calls a blockhead

01:00:36.951 --> 01:00:39.501
is reporting the supernatural
sight his friends saw

01:00:39.501 --> 01:00:41.723
while they were supposed
to be looking for Isabella.

01:00:41.723 --> 01:00:45.678
He says, "we were comprehensive
we might meet a ghost."

01:00:45.678 --> 01:00:47.240
He means apprehensive.

01:00:47.240 --> 01:00:49.513
And there's a playful
irony in the fact that

01:00:49.513 --> 01:00:51.864
because they were apprehensive,
they were totally failing

01:00:51.864 --> 01:00:54.276
to be comprehensive in their search.

01:00:54.276 --> 01:00:55.671
The idea I think is that Jaquez

01:00:55.671 --> 01:00:57.712
has heard apprehensive before.

01:00:57.712 --> 01:00:59.875
He held onto the word and
now tries to work with it.

01:00:59.875 --> 01:01:02.924
But he isn't in control of
the sound and the sense both.

01:01:02.924 --> 01:01:05.526
There's an extra materially
charged irony here

01:01:05.526 --> 01:01:09.295
in that there is an etymological
link between the two words.

01:01:09.295 --> 01:01:11.476
The Latin prehendere , to grasp

01:01:11.476 --> 01:01:14.587
which is exactly what
Jaquez fails at doing.

01:01:14.587 --> 01:01:16.977
Words move, and their
physical parts and meanings

01:01:16.977 --> 01:01:19.887
can shift in the traveling,
grasping and reusing.

01:01:19.887 --> 01:01:22.215
Of course, like Echo Verse and anagram.

01:01:22.215 --> 01:01:25.039
Both the overheard phase
and the clownish malapropism

01:01:25.039 --> 01:01:26.597
have long literary histories.

01:01:26.597 --> 01:01:28.526
But the fact that the
conventions are borrowed

01:01:28.526 --> 01:01:30.725
don't undercut, doesn't undercut

01:01:30.725 --> 01:01:34.184
the way of thinking sound that they offer.

01:01:34.184 --> 01:01:37.002
If the sounds of words
are wayward materials

01:01:37.002 --> 01:01:38.690
depending on prehendere, suggests that

01:01:38.690 --> 01:01:41.009
they are also historical materials.

01:01:41.009 --> 01:01:42.732
Walpole often uses obsolete

01:01:42.732 --> 01:01:44.758
or archaic forms of English words.

01:01:44.758 --> 01:01:47.028
The Broadview edition flags disculpate,

01:01:47.028 --> 01:01:50.113
hallidane, wannat and corse for corpse.

01:01:50.113 --> 01:01:51.863
As well as a whole host of terms

01:01:51.863 --> 01:01:54.253
describing armor and architecture.

01:01:54.253 --> 01:01:56.373
Such terms, like the historical objects

01:01:56.373 --> 01:01:59.303
that some of them reference,
persist from the past.

01:01:59.303 --> 01:02:00.935
And especially with the archaic spellings

01:02:00.935 --> 01:02:01.903
it's particularly their

01:02:01.903 --> 01:02:04.891
corporeal shape as words that persist.

01:02:04.891 --> 01:02:06.862
There are other ways too

01:02:06.862 --> 01:02:08.505
that the novel's words get aligned

01:02:08.505 --> 01:02:10.339
with its historical things.

01:02:10.339 --> 01:02:12.153
Theodore's body is marked

01:02:12.153 --> 01:02:14.914
as Jill and Wendy just nicely reminded us.

01:02:14.914 --> 01:02:16.812
His facial features have
an exact resemblance

01:02:16.812 --> 01:02:18.469
to his ancestor Alphonso

01:02:18.469 --> 01:02:21.034
and his back bears the
mark of the bloody arrow.

01:02:21.034 --> 01:02:24.820
But his name too is the thing
that contains this big secret.

01:02:24.820 --> 01:02:27.657
As scholars have noted, Theodore
comes from the Greek words

01:02:27.657 --> 01:02:30.746
Theos and Dore, it means God given.

01:02:30.746 --> 01:02:33.242
"My name is Theodore," he says climatically

01:02:33.242 --> 01:02:35.906
just before his father recognizes him.

01:02:35.906 --> 01:02:37.653
And in the end, Theodore is both good,

01:02:37.653 --> 01:02:38.986
rightful heir to "Otranto."

01:02:38.986 --> 01:02:42.578
Given "Otranto" by supernatural forces.

01:02:42.578 --> 01:02:44.431
Of course the etymological secret

01:02:44.431 --> 01:02:47.148
is made the more complicated
by the editor's proposal

01:02:47.148 --> 01:02:49.035
that the names of the
novel are fictitious.

01:02:49.035 --> 01:02:50.780
Probably disguised on purpose.

01:02:50.780 --> 01:02:53.355
But whether we think the name
comes from Jerome and his wife

01:02:53.355 --> 01:02:56.831
or from an artful Medieval
writer or from Walpole himself,

01:02:56.831 --> 01:02:59.304
Theodore is the product
of a prior act of naming

01:02:59.304 --> 01:03:02.000
that marks his prior right to "Otranto."

01:03:02.000 --> 01:03:04.668
Certainly, we can't put this
pressure on all of the names.

01:03:04.668 --> 01:03:07.601
But in all of his sound play,
including the eerie echos

01:03:07.601 --> 01:03:10.402
that interest van Elferen in
her account of Gothic sound.

01:03:10.402 --> 01:03:12.350
Walpole throws out possibilities

01:03:12.350 --> 01:03:14.296
without fully developing them.

01:03:14.296 --> 01:03:18.058
In some intriguing ways,
Hallidane wanted Theodore.

01:03:18.058 --> 01:03:20.139
Walpole's words are like echoes.

01:03:20.139 --> 01:03:22.038
Sounds that move meaningfully,

01:03:22.038 --> 01:03:25.167
if unpredictably through space and time.

01:03:25.167 --> 01:03:26.779
Since I've been working here alongside

01:03:26.779 --> 01:03:29.255
but against the grain
of van Elferen's account

01:03:29.255 --> 01:03:30.919
I'll close as she does.

01:03:30.919 --> 01:03:32.946
She notes that for all its talk of sounds

01:03:32.946 --> 01:03:36.368
the page of the Gothic
novel is itself mute.

01:03:36.368 --> 01:03:38.087
Grace Poole's laughter in "Jane Eyre"

01:03:38.087 --> 01:03:39.205
is all the more frightening

01:03:39.205 --> 01:03:41.913
because it is doubly removed
from its alleged origin.

01:03:41.913 --> 01:03:43.747
First through its ghostly disembodiment

01:03:43.747 --> 01:03:46.641
and second, because the reader
does not actually hear it.

01:03:46.641 --> 01:03:48.502
For van Elferen, the mute page

01:03:48.502 --> 01:03:51.941
makes Gothic sounds doubly
immaterial and uncanny.

01:03:51.941 --> 01:03:54.021
This is not I think, how Walpole's

01:03:54.021 --> 01:03:56.584
various plays with the
sounds of words work.

01:03:56.584 --> 01:03:59.822
If anything, repeated phrases,
malapropisms and etymologies

01:03:59.822 --> 01:04:02.430
heighten our sense of the
physical qualities of words.

01:04:02.430 --> 01:04:04.539
In our ears and on the page.

01:04:04.539 --> 01:04:06.046
The sound plays thus seem related

01:04:06.046 --> 01:04:07.731
to other found texts in the novel.

01:04:07.731 --> 01:04:09.913
The couplet written on
the unearthed saber blade.

01:04:09.913 --> 01:04:12.808
The ancient prophecy
difficult to make any sense of

01:04:12.808 --> 01:04:16.144
which is said to have pronounced
that word interests me.

01:04:16.144 --> 01:04:18.569
The downfall of the present family.

01:04:18.569 --> 01:04:21.125
And of course Walpole first
presented his novel itself

01:04:21.125 --> 01:04:22.911
as a found object.

01:04:22.911 --> 01:04:24.727
Like the sounds that
features the novel itself

01:04:24.727 --> 01:04:27.752
as a material historical wayward thing.

01:04:27.752 --> 01:04:30.535
This brings me to a
final question or puzzle.

01:04:30.535 --> 01:04:31.754
I've long been struck by the way

01:04:31.754 --> 01:04:34.132
the sound of the novel changes at the end.

01:04:34.132 --> 01:04:36.848
Throughout, Walpole
favors alliterative pairs

01:04:36.848 --> 01:04:40.564
and pious intentions, reason
to rejoice, they're everywhere.

01:04:40.564 --> 01:04:42.635
But then Manfred stabs Matilda

01:04:42.635 --> 01:04:45.578
and suddenly we get these
intense patches of alliteration

01:04:45.578 --> 01:04:48.185
that feel to me like a departure
from the earlier prose.

01:04:48.185 --> 01:04:49.382
You'll just have to listen here.

01:04:49.382 --> 01:04:52.160
"Murderous monster" cried Manfred.

01:04:52.160 --> 01:04:56.678
"Heaven directed my bloody
hand to the heart of my child.

01:04:56.678 --> 01:04:58.711
"The earth rocked and the clank of

01:04:58.711 --> 01:05:02.096
"more than mortal armor was heard behind.

01:05:02.096 --> 01:05:04.312
"Our doom is pronounced.

01:05:04.312 --> 01:05:06.187
"Shall we not, can we not but dedicate

01:05:06.187 --> 01:05:09.052
"the few deplorable hours we have to live

01:05:09.052 --> 01:05:12.208
"in deprecating the
farther wrath of Heaven."

01:05:12.208 --> 01:05:15.128
You should have these written
out, so I don't have to look.

01:05:15.128 --> 01:05:16.972
I truly don't know what to make of these.

01:05:16.972 --> 01:05:18.364
The alliteration amps up

01:05:18.364 --> 01:05:20.426
right as the drama reaches its crisis.

01:05:20.426 --> 01:05:21.709
So we might think of something

01:05:21.709 --> 01:05:23.257
like what Garrett Stewart sees

01:05:23.257 --> 01:05:24.818
as carefully knotted linguistic

01:05:24.818 --> 01:05:27.221
and psychological threads in "Jane Eyre."

01:05:27.221 --> 01:05:29.364
But murderous monster and Manfred

01:05:29.364 --> 01:05:31.157
come from different voices.

01:05:31.157 --> 01:05:33.707
And I'm not sure that there's
any necessary relation

01:05:33.707 --> 01:05:36.032
between alliteration and emotion.

01:05:36.032 --> 01:05:38.836
In fact, one might think
that in moments of intensity

01:05:38.836 --> 01:05:42.614
the mediating material of
text might seem to fall away.

01:05:42.614 --> 01:05:45.826
There is an onomatopoeia in
the clank of supernatural armor

01:05:45.826 --> 01:05:47.737
but this isn't consistent either.

01:05:47.737 --> 01:05:50.008
For how do breathy Hs fit stabbing?

01:05:50.008 --> 01:05:53.142
Or the drum beat of Ds a pious resolution.

01:05:53.142 --> 01:05:54.895
I haven't quite figured
out the logic of this

01:05:54.895 --> 01:05:57.281
but I'm tempted to think about
these alliterative bursts

01:05:57.281 --> 01:06:00.070
in relationship to the
Echo Verse disaggregation

01:06:00.070 --> 01:06:04.894
of T E A R S, or the
anagrammatic play of Muralto.

01:06:04.894 --> 01:06:06.997
Relevant too, is the argument

01:06:06.997 --> 01:06:09.317
recurrent in "Prosody" after Milton

01:06:09.317 --> 01:06:12.251
that there is something
distinctively Gothic about rhyme.

01:06:12.251 --> 01:06:14.686
"The invention of a barbarous age."

01:06:14.686 --> 01:06:16.797
Especially and so far as it involves

01:06:16.797 --> 01:06:19.724
trivial play with jingling words.

01:06:19.724 --> 01:06:22.320
Like the castle walls
crumbling behind Manfred.

01:06:22.320 --> 01:06:24.207
Words are material and mortal.

01:06:24.207 --> 01:06:28.069
Fallen, fallible, emotion-evoking
and anyway all we got.

01:06:28.069 --> 01:06:29.738
When Walpole uses alliteration

01:06:29.738 --> 01:06:32.409
to emphasize the letters of
his words at the novel's end.

01:06:32.409 --> 01:06:34.017
Might he again be embracing the fact

01:06:34.017 --> 01:06:35.476
that words are made of sounds

01:06:35.476 --> 01:06:37.769
embody material phenomena that combine,

01:06:37.769 --> 01:06:41.364
disaggregate, recombine,
persist echoing one another.

01:06:41.364 --> 01:06:42.404
Thanks.

01:06:42.404 --> 01:06:45.404
(audience clapping)

01:06:54.069 --> 01:06:56.352
- Open it to questions.

01:06:56.352 --> 01:06:58.769
- I have questions for the two papers on sound.

01:07:00.548 --> 01:07:02.288
I love the analogy to film.

01:07:02.288 --> 01:07:04.882
But I was wondering why you need film

01:07:04.882 --> 01:07:07.244
when there is such a pronounced

01:07:07.244 --> 01:07:10.769
and interesting tradition
of achieving stage effects

01:07:10.769 --> 01:07:12.186
by unreal sounds?

01:07:13.383 --> 01:07:15.261
So I know Garrick talks about how

01:07:15.261 --> 01:07:19.586
the player who plays Macbeth
should wear cork in his shoes

01:07:19.586 --> 01:07:22.161
so that he can walk
across the stage silently

01:07:22.161 --> 01:07:23.987
for the murder scene.

01:07:23.987 --> 01:07:26.478
It seems that many of the
things that you're describing

01:07:26.478 --> 01:07:28.458
seem to be a consequence of the thing

01:07:28.458 --> 01:07:29.702
that for me is paramount,

01:07:29.702 --> 01:07:33.869
that this is a translation of
a five-act drama into a novel.

01:07:35.204 --> 01:07:38.435
So would be interested to quickly add that

01:07:38.435 --> 01:07:40.133
I love the concept of echoes

01:07:40.133 --> 01:07:42.392
and I mean you should think about

01:07:42.392 --> 01:07:45.351
I mean the notion of character
echos in the Gothic.

01:07:45.351 --> 01:07:48.759
So when do we hear that the name
Theodore in "The Monk"

01:07:48.759 --> 01:07:51.781
that is meant to echo that
character Theodore that we get

01:07:51.781 --> 01:07:53.614
in "Castle of Otranto." (indistinct)

01:07:54.633 --> 01:07:58.983
I see what it is in texts
like "The Monk," Ambrosio

01:07:58.983 --> 01:08:03.250
seems echo to Antonio which tells us about

01:08:03.250 --> 01:08:05.167
the connection as well.

01:08:07.664 --> 01:08:08.808
- Go ahead.

01:08:08.808 --> 01:08:10.873
- Yeah, that's great, that's super useful.

01:08:10.873 --> 01:08:15.040
And I like the way then that
the character itself is being,

01:08:16.805 --> 01:08:19.502
like the name is the kind of
material metonymy for that

01:08:19.502 --> 01:08:21.214
and then it's picked up out of the text.

01:08:21.214 --> 01:08:22.515
So there's something kind of

01:08:22.515 --> 01:08:25.303
portable and trinkety
or something about that.

01:08:25.303 --> 01:08:27.801
I'll have to do more
thinking, that's great.

01:08:27.801 --> 01:08:28.634
- John.

01:08:28.634 --> 01:08:31.955
Do you want to pick up the
other part of the question?

01:08:31.955 --> 01:08:35.372
I believe in both and I wouldn't rule out

01:08:36.930 --> 01:08:39.281
the things from the 18th-century theater.

01:08:39.281 --> 01:08:41.694
But I do think there's a value added

01:08:41.694 --> 01:08:44.444
in the sound theorist because the

01:08:45.294 --> 01:08:48.794
notions like the psycho acoustic contract.

01:08:49.711 --> 01:08:52.294
And the acousto-sonic contract

01:08:53.976 --> 01:08:57.764
in which you get a
multiplication or a magnification

01:08:57.764 --> 01:09:01.882
of the effect by the presentness of both.

01:09:01.882 --> 01:09:05.632
Now that could happen
in the theater as well.

01:09:07.672 --> 01:09:10.839
But I think also the methodical use of

01:09:17.397 --> 01:09:20.480
real sounds in the Foley artist's lab

01:09:22.231 --> 01:09:23.898
to make effects that

01:09:25.963 --> 01:09:30.388
are either on the one hand
like those sounds in film

01:09:30.388 --> 01:09:32.596
or the use of completely different objects

01:09:32.596 --> 01:09:35.587
to make sounds that become real.

01:09:35.587 --> 01:09:37.669
Probably we could find analogues in theater

01:09:37.669 --> 01:09:41.669
but I think it's much
more vivid in film theory.

01:09:44.211 --> 01:09:48.378
I don't see any harm, I guess
and I do see value added.

01:09:49.926 --> 01:09:52.946
But the historical dimension
from the 18th-century theater

01:09:52.946 --> 01:09:54.627
would be great as well.

01:09:54.627 --> 01:09:56.452
And of course Walpole was involved in it

01:09:56.452 --> 01:09:59.829
and Marshall Brown writes about the

01:09:59.829 --> 01:10:03.488
effects of the Gothic in
plays prior to Walpole.

01:10:03.488 --> 01:10:06.238
So I don't doubt that it's there.

01:10:07.286 --> 01:10:08.672
- I just, to follow up
with a quick question

01:10:08.672 --> 01:10:11.668
which is not "Otranto"
based, but just Foley based

01:10:11.668 --> 01:10:13.524
which I didn't know
anything about until today.

01:10:13.524 --> 01:10:16.381
Are those sound dubbers as it were,

01:10:16.381 --> 01:10:19.432
are they watching the
film as they do this?

01:10:19.432 --> 01:10:21.391
They're not following a script?

01:10:21.391 --> 01:10:22.237
- No, they're watching the film.

01:10:22.237 --> 01:10:24.227
- They're watching, okay so
it's visual as they're doing.

01:10:24.227 --> 01:10:25.060
Cindy?

01:10:25.060 --> 01:10:26.874
- Well this speaks to the value added

01:10:26.874 --> 01:10:29.026
that you just mentioned John.

01:10:29.026 --> 01:10:31.993
And I think this could
bring you in too, Courtney.

01:10:31.993 --> 01:10:35.313
But what your Foley
effects made me think of.

01:10:35.313 --> 01:10:39.317
You said the audio visual
contract applies to everything

01:10:39.317 --> 01:10:41.297
kind of except dialogue.

01:10:41.297 --> 01:10:43.880
But Joe's reading made me think

01:10:44.891 --> 01:10:47.864
in "Otranto," practicing
the Gothic more generally.

01:10:47.864 --> 01:10:50.157
The dialogue itself is laid on.

01:10:50.157 --> 01:10:54.074
That's one of the things
that Lewis didn't like.

01:10:55.036 --> 01:10:57.478
He said how could this
be realistic dialogue.

01:10:57.478 --> 01:10:59.371
And then he quoted some of these lines.

01:10:59.371 --> 01:11:00.767
(audience laughs)

01:11:00.767 --> 01:11:02.542
Even then they didn't
talk like this, did they?

01:11:02.542 --> 01:11:05.128
So there is a kind of sonic overlay

01:11:05.128 --> 01:11:09.513
in the text itself, and the
Foley effect of the dialogue.

01:11:09.513 --> 01:11:11.783
- Yeah I can see that, yes.

01:11:11.783 --> 01:11:15.450
There is sometimes
dubbed dialogue in films.

01:11:16.569 --> 01:11:20.736
But in general, the dialogue
will have been recorded on set

01:11:21.679 --> 01:11:24.263
but there are many, many instances
where sound is dubbed in.

01:11:24.263 --> 01:11:27.180
(Indistinct) Most obviously singers in musicals.

01:11:31.023 --> 01:11:31.856
- Yeah?

01:11:31.856 --> 01:11:34.331
- Alliteration sounds louder
than they're actually talking.

01:11:34.331 --> 01:11:35.164
- Yes. Sandra.

01:11:37.039 --> 01:11:38.956
- So I was hoping Sarah

01:11:40.501 --> 01:11:44.668
that you and Sean might be able
to talk about the arabesque

01:11:46.002 --> 01:11:50.169
because on Sean's account,
like ornamentality is a kind of

01:11:54.858 --> 01:11:58.949
multiplication of, yeah
kind of multiplicity

01:11:58.949 --> 01:12:01.363
or mode of multiplication.

01:12:01.363 --> 01:12:03.780
Whereas for you, this idea of

01:12:04.915 --> 01:12:07.810
the border exerting its own agency

01:12:07.810 --> 01:12:11.977
seems different from that
account of the arabesque

01:12:13.506 --> 01:12:15.888
"as its multiplicity. Right"

01:12:15.888 --> 01:12:18.909
So that was my sense of their being,

01:12:18.909 --> 01:12:23.303
a kind of different logic
to each of your accounts

01:12:23.303 --> 01:12:26.738
of something that on its
face might be understood.

01:12:26.738 --> 01:12:30.181
Even in mainstream aesthetics
in their own period as similar.

01:12:30.181 --> 01:12:33.681
So I'm just throwing that out there.

01:12:33.681 --> 01:12:35.949
- I mean, the arabesque isn't a term

01:12:35.949 --> 01:12:39.899
I've thought a huge amount
before writing this paper.

01:12:39.899 --> 01:12:42.732
So I think what was striking to me

01:12:44.840 --> 01:12:48.553
in the characterizations
of it that I was reading

01:12:48.553 --> 01:12:50.895
was that it was on the one hand

01:12:50.895 --> 01:12:54.771
sort of picking up on the
idea of the serpentine,

01:12:54.771 --> 01:12:57.932
was characteristic of the arabesque.

01:12:57.932 --> 01:13:01.267
But that there were also these,

01:13:01.267 --> 01:13:03.934
that the term shellwork is used.

01:13:05.669 --> 01:13:07.673
And even although Kant doesn't talk

01:13:07.673 --> 01:13:10.139
or use the term arabesque
but he talks about shellwork

01:13:10.139 --> 01:13:13.972
and describes formations
that people think of,

01:13:14.893 --> 01:13:17.560
could be described as arabesque.

01:13:18.782 --> 01:13:22.949
But I definitely think I
don't want to make the claim

01:13:23.830 --> 01:13:27.997
that it's just one dimensional
or linear and not complex.

01:13:29.008 --> 01:13:32.817
The thing I was struck by more
was that if you hear me say.

01:13:32.817 --> 01:13:34.109
Oh, mine is about thread

01:13:34.109 --> 01:13:36.307
and then yours is about
weaving and complexity.

01:13:36.307 --> 01:13:38.281
It sounds like I'm setting up a kind of

01:13:38.281 --> 01:13:41.123
opposition that I don't really,
I don't want to do that.

01:13:41.123 --> 01:13:44.412
And I'm getting the language
of the thread and the shell,

01:13:44.412 --> 01:13:46.964
I'm getting from Hogarth where he

01:13:46.964 --> 01:13:48.716
as you know, I'm sure many of you know

01:13:48.716 --> 01:13:51.133
he talks about the threads as

01:13:52.065 --> 01:13:55.260
the shell is made up of
a network of threads.

01:13:55.260 --> 01:13:58.163
So it is a kind of complex

01:13:58.163 --> 01:14:02.330
and he's not trying to make
it into a binary opposition

01:14:03.525 --> 01:14:06.545
between two completely distinct forms.

01:14:06.545 --> 01:14:09.692
So I think there's room for both of those.

01:14:09.692 --> 01:14:13.359
The emphasis on bordering
and on complexity.

01:14:14.427 --> 01:14:18.594
- But then the pelvic bones
are kind of exemplar of

01:14:21.574 --> 01:14:24.157
ornamentation of the arabesque.

01:14:25.098 --> 01:14:27.631
And there's something about pelvic bones, that are

01:14:27.631 --> 01:14:30.214
with respect to the human body,

01:14:31.839 --> 01:14:35.089
the very idea of reduction say in your vocabulary

01:14:40.436 --> 01:14:43.769
So the carriage, the under, the irreducible.

01:14:51.251 --> 01:14:54.918
And yeah, the irreducible
basis of the form.

01:15:00.904 --> 01:15:03.009
- I wasn't ready for the pelvic bone.

01:15:03.009 --> 01:15:05.842
(audience laughs)

01:15:07.256 --> 01:15:08.713
- No one ever is Sean.

01:15:08.713 --> 01:15:11.713
(audience laughing)

01:15:13.055 --> 01:15:13.888
- Alright.

01:15:13.888 --> 01:15:16.527
- Pelvic bone, is that from filming?

01:15:16.527 --> 01:15:17.738
What is that from?

01:15:17.738 --> 01:15:19.836
- The analysis that there's the.

01:15:19.836 --> 01:15:21.586
You know, this shape.

01:15:29.461 --> 01:15:32.226
- Right, right, right, right.

01:15:32.226 --> 01:15:34.893
I do, I think I understand, I think.

01:15:34.893 --> 01:15:35.814
- There's a clear way.

01:15:35.814 --> 01:15:36.837
And this is probably tangential.

01:15:36.837 --> 01:15:39.177
But there's a clear way in which Hogarth.

01:15:39.177 --> 01:15:43.623
It's quite clear to me that
he's responding to a discourse

01:15:43.623 --> 01:15:46.470
that emerges in the late 17th century

01:15:46.470 --> 01:15:48.065
about the worldliness of things.

01:15:48.065 --> 01:15:50.288
And that turns up in its own way

01:15:50.288 --> 01:15:51.701
when he starts thinking seriously about

01:15:51.701 --> 01:15:54.201
what it means to do engraving.

01:15:55.395 --> 01:15:58.556
And he stumbles over the question of

01:15:58.556 --> 01:16:00.588
the fact that engraving looks like,

01:16:00.588 --> 01:16:02.968
looks unfortunately like texture.

01:16:02.968 --> 01:16:05.467
But he also wants to get
to a sense of how is it

01:16:05.467 --> 01:16:09.199
that individual lines add up to surface.

01:16:09.199 --> 01:16:11.531
And that's the fun, that's one of the ways

01:16:11.531 --> 01:16:13.922
that late 18th-century philosophy

01:16:13.922 --> 01:16:16.046
comes to the notion of
emergent properties.

01:16:16.046 --> 01:16:17.352
How you can take individual lines

01:16:17.352 --> 01:16:19.094
and add them up to surface.

01:16:19.094 --> 01:16:22.358
So I mean that's what I see as being the,

01:16:22.358 --> 01:16:23.608
as a root from.

01:16:26.300 --> 01:16:28.675
The root from thread to shell in Hogarth

01:16:28.675 --> 01:16:29.665
as he's working it out.

01:16:29.665 --> 01:16:30.498
- Yeah.

01:16:30.498 --> 01:16:31.883
- There's a different
dimension that has to do with

01:16:31.883 --> 01:16:34.069
specifically the serpentine

01:16:34.069 --> 01:16:36.326
that has to do with returning
to embodied movement

01:16:36.326 --> 01:16:39.993
because that's the line
that occupies space.

01:16:44.362 --> 01:16:45.569
- I have a question.

01:16:45.569 --> 01:16:47.767
But I wanted to state
something real quickly

01:16:47.767 --> 01:16:50.643
with the shell thread conversation.

01:16:50.643 --> 01:16:54.334
This is something that Sarah
and I have talked about before

01:16:54.334 --> 01:16:58.200
but I'm struck in the context
of the Gothic and "Otranto"

01:16:58.200 --> 01:17:00.332
by the fact that Hogarth in the moment

01:17:00.332 --> 01:17:04.360
that he wants to turn
the shell into a metaphor

01:17:04.360 --> 01:17:07.775
for this linear figure made of threads.

01:17:07.775 --> 01:17:09.573
His phrasing is, he says like

01:17:09.573 --> 01:17:13.869
imagine a shell with its
contents scooped out so nicely.

01:17:13.869 --> 01:17:14.967
As I've talked to Sarah before

01:17:14.967 --> 01:17:18.050
about how I find this phrase so gory.

01:17:19.361 --> 01:17:20.512
(everyone laughs)

01:17:20.512 --> 01:17:21.425
And evocative.

01:17:21.425 --> 01:17:22.954
But then he actually has to go through

01:17:22.954 --> 01:17:26.781
the motions of imagining
scooping out the material,

01:17:26.781 --> 01:17:30.354
organic contents of an
actual biological shell.

01:17:30.354 --> 01:17:32.327
Right, that has a creature inside.

01:17:32.327 --> 01:17:36.494
In order to turn it into a
figure of just threads and lines.

01:17:38.728 --> 01:17:40.719
And anyway, so there's something,

01:17:40.719 --> 01:17:42.341
there's a kind of haunting,

01:17:42.341 --> 01:17:43.876
there's this kind of gory haunting.

01:17:43.876 --> 01:17:45.819
- And I know you have another question.

01:17:45.819 --> 01:17:46.860
But it just makes me think

01:17:46.860 --> 01:17:49.500
that they would have had to
scoop out the helmet presumably.

01:17:49.500 --> 01:17:50.333
- Right?

01:17:51.575 --> 01:17:55.233
- Because otherwise they would have been.

01:17:55.233 --> 01:17:56.066
- Oh I see.

01:17:56.066 --> 01:17:57.707
- Because it had Conrad stuck to it.

01:17:57.707 --> 01:17:59.282
- All his remains were there, right?

01:17:59.282 --> 01:18:00.229
- Right, yeah.

01:18:00.229 --> 01:18:02.698
But I actually have a question

01:18:02.698 --> 01:18:05.892
inspired by Courtney's
discussion of echo.

01:18:05.892 --> 01:18:09.940
And I want to bring Courtney's
and Wendy's papers together.

01:18:09.940 --> 01:18:11.107
I'm gonna try.

01:18:12.094 --> 01:18:14.111
So I don't know if you've
already thought about this.

01:18:14.111 --> 01:18:15.459
But when you were describing,

01:18:15.459 --> 01:18:17.311
when you were doing your reading of echoes

01:18:17.311 --> 01:18:20.262
and particularly the Echo Verse.

01:18:20.262 --> 01:18:23.095
Where the echo is this reiteration

01:18:24.787 --> 01:18:28.398
with a very minor, formal distortion

01:18:28.398 --> 01:18:31.292
that has an enormous consequence

01:18:31.292 --> 01:18:34.048
in terms of the meaning
or the significance

01:18:34.048 --> 01:18:35.548
of the utterance.

01:18:36.834 --> 01:18:39.311
It seemed to describe so perfectly

01:18:39.311 --> 01:18:41.737
the epigraph to "The Castle of Otranto."

01:18:41.737 --> 01:18:44.146
The little passage from Horace

01:18:44.146 --> 01:18:47.110
that I think Sean made reference to

01:18:47.110 --> 01:18:48.832
in his paper this morning.

01:18:48.832 --> 01:18:53.095
But where he misquotes
Horace from the "Ars Poetica."

01:18:53.095 --> 01:18:55.829
And it's a very, very minor tweak, right?

01:18:55.829 --> 01:18:59.996
Like he alters the language
in this really minor way

01:19:01.115 --> 01:19:03.186
but completely reverses the meaning.

01:19:03.186 --> 01:19:05.763
So the "Ars Poetica" kind of says

01:19:05.763 --> 01:19:09.475
in the grotesque or the ridiculous.

01:19:09.475 --> 01:19:12.907
The head and the foot
cannot be reconciled to.

01:19:12.907 --> 01:19:14.399
You know, there's strange fantasies

01:19:14.399 --> 01:19:15.489
where the head and foot

01:19:15.489 --> 01:19:17.751
cannot be reconciled to a single form.

01:19:17.751 --> 01:19:20.193
And the epigraph to "Otranto" says

01:19:20.193 --> 01:19:22.531
strange, strange fantasies in which

01:19:22.531 --> 01:19:24.627
the head and the foot are nevertheless

01:19:24.627 --> 01:19:26.960
reconciled to a single form.

01:19:29.269 --> 01:19:32.269
So this reminded me of Wendy's claim

01:19:35.479 --> 01:19:36.972
that I completely agree with.

01:19:36.972 --> 01:19:38.363
That there's something about camp

01:19:38.363 --> 01:19:41.030
that redeems heterosexual forms.

01:19:43.684 --> 01:19:46.203
There's something about that echo

01:19:46.203 --> 01:19:48.978
and the distortion it performs

01:19:48.978 --> 01:19:53.145
that nevertheless gives a
form or restores to a form.

01:19:54.041 --> 01:19:58.208
It's like camp distort is an
echo of this horrible original

01:20:00.004 --> 01:20:02.024
that distorts it in such a way

01:20:02.024 --> 01:20:05.813
that it actually provides
a redeemed form for it.

01:20:05.813 --> 01:20:10.102
Like it gives it a redeemed
form to go forward.

01:20:10.102 --> 01:20:13.201
- By redeem I meant like,
not want to kill yourself.

01:20:13.201 --> 01:20:15.069
(everyone laughs)

01:20:15.069 --> 01:20:18.152
- But that's kind of what I mean too.

01:20:19.276 --> 01:20:22.276
But camp or parody or whatever it is

01:20:23.683 --> 01:20:27.079
as a kind of echo in this sense.

01:20:27.079 --> 01:20:28.453
I guess I'm just wondering.

01:20:28.453 --> 01:20:32.370
If that's at all a different
way of thinking about it?

01:20:33.551 --> 01:20:36.866
Or if echo is just a term
I've not thought about

01:20:36.866 --> 01:20:39.128
that I'm kind of making describe

01:20:39.128 --> 01:20:42.309
things like camp and parody
the way we've always,

01:20:42.309 --> 01:20:45.287
or we've been describing
them for a long time.

01:20:45.287 --> 01:20:46.430
- I mean that's great.

01:20:46.430 --> 01:20:47.841
The only thing that comes to mind

01:20:47.841 --> 01:20:52.008
is the thing with the partial
because you take off the T.

01:20:53.296 --> 01:20:57.463
So whereas I think camp
is like the whole shebang.

01:20:58.746 --> 01:21:00.400
Not the partial.

01:21:00.400 --> 01:21:01.854
But you know.

01:21:01.854 --> 01:21:03.490
- I have a sort of follow up question
that pertains to it.

01:21:03.490 --> 01:21:07.542
which was you know, the alliterative stuff

01:21:07.542 --> 01:21:09.297
that you were reading at the end there.

01:21:09.297 --> 01:21:10.844
And your YouTube video.

01:21:10.844 --> 01:21:13.552
I mean they're both very camp.

01:21:13.552 --> 01:21:16.822
And it made me think about Pope's thing

01:21:16.822 --> 01:21:19.354
about "the sound must seem
an echo to the sense."

01:21:19.354 --> 01:21:21.323
Which I also have never understood

01:21:21.323 --> 01:21:23.748
except at the most nominal level.

01:21:23.748 --> 01:21:27.049
And it made me wonder whether
he's talking about the

01:21:27.049 --> 01:21:28.716
affective and formal

01:21:32.155 --> 01:21:33.988
life of camp actually.

01:21:35.614 --> 01:21:36.998
Which sort of explains why it's

01:21:36.998 --> 01:21:38.939
"the sound must seem an echo to the sense."

01:21:38.939 --> 01:21:41.911
So I get what you're saying
that it's not partial.

01:21:41.911 --> 01:21:46.241
It's beyond, it's like
more than not less than.

01:21:46.241 --> 01:21:47.142
- Right.

01:21:47.142 --> 01:21:48.068
- But yeah.

01:21:48.068 --> 01:21:50.240
- I would say like in echo
verse that also happens.

01:21:50.240 --> 01:21:51.462
Like other ones say like.

01:21:51.462 --> 01:21:55.212
Oh "What do women most prize
when we men address?"

01:21:57.286 --> 01:21:58.339
Like it ends with "a dress."

01:21:58.339 --> 01:21:59.307
Like what do.

01:21:59.307 --> 01:22:02.598
And then what women most
prize is a space dress.

01:22:02.598 --> 01:22:03.824
- Right?
- Right.

01:22:03.824 --> 01:22:06.658
- Or even just the ears
and then ears comes back.

01:22:06.658 --> 01:22:08.843
So I think there is a way.

01:22:08.843 --> 01:22:10.167
I don't know what you think about this.

01:22:10.167 --> 01:22:12.208
But I guess that I would think that

01:22:12.208 --> 01:22:14.035
one of the things Echo Verse does is just

01:22:14.035 --> 01:22:16.865
throw attention onto like

01:22:16.865 --> 01:22:19.497
the matter that's bringing it to us.

01:22:19.497 --> 01:22:23.303
Right, or it brings attention
to the medium in a certain way

01:22:23.303 --> 01:22:25.521
which you're reading did exactly as well.

01:22:25.521 --> 01:22:27.032
Just kind of bring attention

01:22:27.032 --> 01:22:30.199
to the wroughtness of the words right?

01:22:32.617 --> 01:22:34.665
This is like half thought.

01:22:34.665 --> 01:22:37.000
But I was thinking Sophie
actually of your paper

01:22:37.000 --> 01:22:40.333
and the tension and the contrast between

01:22:41.974 --> 01:22:44.149
belief and action, right.

01:22:44.149 --> 01:22:45.443
Maybe there's a way in which

01:22:45.443 --> 01:22:47.848
by just throwing
attention onto the bodily,

01:22:47.848 --> 01:22:51.228
onto the medium, onto the corporeal side

01:22:51.228 --> 01:22:54.799
it just sort of undercuts earnest belief

01:22:54.799 --> 01:22:56.566
like true and spiritual.

01:22:56.566 --> 01:22:58.064
It's not like they really think

01:22:58.064 --> 01:23:00.223
in those sort of overwrought terms.

01:23:00.223 --> 01:23:04.393
But there's a kind of awareness
that deflates a little bit.

01:23:04.393 --> 01:23:06.860
- So John is this really
different than a Foley effect?

01:23:06.860 --> 01:23:08.723
Because on the one hand, a Foley effect

01:23:08.723 --> 01:23:11.799
more real than real but it's not.

01:23:11.799 --> 01:23:14.799
And it's emphasizing the materiality

01:23:15.773 --> 01:23:18.058
in the key turning or the foot stomping.

01:23:18.058 --> 01:23:20.536
But to completely mask, I mean the effect

01:23:20.536 --> 01:23:22.162
is pure reality in other words.

01:23:22.162 --> 01:23:22.995
You're not?

01:23:22.995 --> 01:23:23.979
- In our perception of it.

01:23:23.979 --> 01:23:25.621
- Yeah.
- Yes.

01:23:25.621 --> 01:23:26.660
- It's not camp.

01:23:26.660 --> 01:23:29.074
Foley effect is not camp.

01:23:29.074 --> 01:23:32.094
- Well not if it's effective.

01:23:32.094 --> 01:23:33.860
(audience laughs)

01:23:33.860 --> 01:23:35.881
- I mean because then it seems to be

01:23:35.881 --> 01:23:39.214
part of a kind of natural world of the film.

01:23:40.139 --> 01:23:41.656
- But in that clip it is.

01:23:41.656 --> 01:23:42.489
In the clip it is.

01:23:42.489 --> 01:23:44.237
- Yes I was thinking of that too.

01:23:44.237 --> 01:23:48.404
- There you got the film making
a meta comment on itself.

01:23:50.190 --> 01:23:51.504
I had a question for you.

01:23:51.504 --> 01:23:53.251
Could I quickly go back to Jesse's.

01:23:53.251 --> 01:23:57.781
There was a medium other than
theater in the 18th century.

01:23:57.781 --> 01:23:59.749
Which was phantasmagoria.

01:23:59.749 --> 01:24:01.102
Which really comes into its own

01:24:01.102 --> 01:24:02.341
in the later part of the century.

01:24:02.341 --> 01:24:06.100
But there were sound
effects in that as well.

01:24:06.100 --> 01:24:07.567
But Wendy, I wonder.

01:24:07.567 --> 01:24:09.650
Maybe I missed something, but

01:24:10.966 --> 01:24:14.716
Do you believe that
"Otranto" was camp in 1764?

01:24:19.863 --> 01:24:21.946
Or is it only camp later?

01:24:23.322 --> 01:24:24.489
Including now.

01:24:25.517 --> 01:24:26.350
- I don't know.

01:24:26.350 --> 01:24:30.536
It seems like it always had
to be camp but I don't know.

01:24:30.536 --> 01:24:32.384
I mean I guess camp.

01:24:32.384 --> 01:24:35.218
- A lot of people were
scared to death by it.

01:24:35.218 --> 01:24:37.802
There's a story about
people going out on a boat

01:24:37.802 --> 01:24:39.412
and reading it by candlelight.

01:24:39.412 --> 01:24:41.834
- Oh I guess I was thinking
was it camp for Walpole?

01:24:41.834 --> 01:24:43.440
What do you think the answer to that is?

01:24:43.440 --> 01:24:44.321
I mean this is.

01:24:44.321 --> 01:24:46.083
- Well there are parts of it I guess.

01:24:46.083 --> 01:24:47.343
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

01:24:47.343 --> 01:24:50.251
- Not just this screwball stuff 
in the dialogue

01:24:50.251 --> 01:24:53.137
but even at the very beginning.

01:24:53.137 --> 01:24:55.243
- Well if you go back to Pope.

01:24:55.243 --> 01:24:56.076
- That's scary.

01:24:56.076 --> 01:24:57.698
- If you go back to the Scriblerian

01:24:57.698 --> 01:25:00.481
"Peri Hupsous," "The Art of Sinking in Poetry"

01:25:00.481 --> 01:25:02.491
which we were talking about.

01:25:02.491 --> 01:25:05.887
And I mean there's, if not camp

01:25:05.887 --> 01:25:09.887
certainly a sendup element
of the faults sublime

01:25:12.846 --> 01:25:14.873
already there in whenever that was written,

01:25:14.873 --> 01:25:18.851
1720 or 18 or 21 or something like that.

01:25:18.851 --> 01:25:20.466
But that group of people.

01:25:20.466 --> 01:25:24.510
So that notion of a sendup of the serious

01:25:24.510 --> 01:25:27.025
is certainly already there.

01:25:27.025 --> 01:25:30.942
And their mock panegyrics
and other mock forms.

01:25:32.100 --> 01:25:34.295
"Tale of the Tub" is brimming with them.

01:25:34.295 --> 01:25:36.378
Is that the same as camp?

01:25:37.221 --> 01:25:38.708
I'm just not sure, I think.

01:25:38.708 --> 01:25:40.203
- Yeah no I don't think quite.

01:25:40.203 --> 01:25:41.703
- I think Macaulay talking about

01:25:41.703 --> 01:25:43.695
Walpole's taste in antiquaries

01:25:43.695 --> 01:25:47.905
but maybe also "The Castle of
Otranto" uses the term, the odd

01:25:47.905 --> 01:25:52.072
which I always thought was a
kind of good expression of.

01:25:53.198 --> 01:25:56.573
Maybe that's not quite the camp
and it's not quite the novel

01:25:56.573 --> 01:25:59.459
but it's something a little off.

01:25:59.459 --> 01:26:01.864
- It's not snarky, you know.

01:26:01.864 --> 01:26:03.614
I mean, you say more.

01:26:04.467 --> 01:26:08.333
Camp is really, like it's only
doing the Donna Summer thing

01:26:08.333 --> 01:26:10.793
because it's like weeping all the time.

01:26:10.793 --> 01:26:13.876
It's like so sad, camp is really sad.

01:26:14.738 --> 01:26:18.877
And it's just choosing
not to be sad about it.

01:26:18.877 --> 01:26:19.993
I don't know.

01:26:19.993 --> 01:26:24.160
- You said it's the, when
you said it's the wraparound.

01:26:25.578 --> 01:26:27.776
It's tears without ears.

01:26:27.776 --> 01:26:30.301
(everyone laughs)

01:26:30.301 --> 01:26:31.688
- Or ears with it.

01:26:31.688 --> 01:26:34.313
I mean if it's sad but.

01:26:34.313 --> 01:26:36.574
Wait, what did you just say?

01:26:36.574 --> 01:26:40.387
It's sad without being sad about the sad.

01:26:40.387 --> 01:26:42.475
- I don't know, yeah.

01:26:42.475 --> 01:26:44.910
- Then it would be more
like ears without tears.

01:26:44.910 --> 01:26:46.433
- Ears without tears, yeah.

01:26:46.433 --> 01:26:47.266
- Right.

01:26:50.396 --> 01:26:52.255
- Put it in a question mark.

01:26:52.255 --> 01:26:53.626
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

01:26:53.626 --> 01:26:54.459
- Exactly.

01:26:54.459 --> 01:26:56.237
- Quotation mark with a question mark.

01:26:56.237 --> 01:26:58.030
Because I say the sky is blue

01:26:58.030 --> 01:27:00.583
with an exclamation
point, it's surprising.

01:27:00.583 --> 01:27:03.500
- If you jump off from Sontag then.

01:27:05.063 --> 01:27:07.452
Her ideas, I recall it from years ago.

01:27:07.452 --> 01:27:11.207
Is that there's fundamentally
always some other reference.

01:27:11.207 --> 01:27:15.734
That there's a jump off in
the camp from something else.

01:27:15.734 --> 01:27:19.901
And what's the something
else if it was camp in 1764?

01:27:21.583 --> 01:27:23.168
- What's the something else?

01:27:23.168 --> 01:27:25.424
I mean, patriarchy.

01:27:25.424 --> 01:27:28.886
I mean it's the script in which,

01:27:28.886 --> 01:27:32.391
the overreach of authority, that script.

01:27:32.391 --> 01:27:35.058
Or the meta-novelistic script of

01:27:36.014 --> 01:27:39.960
the peering into the recesses
of your heart script.

01:27:39.960 --> 01:27:41.436
I mean, I don't know.

01:27:41.436 --> 01:27:42.423
Were you thinking?

01:27:42.423 --> 01:27:45.594
- I would follow him, he's
quite explicit on the topic.

01:27:45.594 --> 01:27:46.427
- Oh say.

01:27:46.427 --> 01:27:47.885
- It's the Fielding Richardson novel.

01:27:47.885 --> 01:27:48.833
- Yeah.

01:27:48.833 --> 01:27:50.475
- He's tired of it.

01:27:50.475 --> 01:27:52.535
It doesn't have enough in it.

01:27:52.535 --> 01:27:54.365
It doesn't have fantasy in it.

01:27:54.365 --> 01:27:55.198
- Yeah.

01:27:55.198 --> 01:27:57.087
- That's what's being setup, I think.

01:27:57.087 --> 01:27:59.263
Whether you call it camp or not.

01:27:59.263 --> 01:28:02.751
It's that kind of high
novel that we so adore.

01:28:02.751 --> 01:28:03.584
- Yeah.

01:28:03.584 --> 01:28:04.806
- Is what he's sending up.

01:28:04.806 --> 01:28:07.975
He says it right at the preface.

01:28:07.975 --> 01:28:09.636
- But you make this brilliant point John

01:28:09.636 --> 01:28:12.436
that has been so influential to me.

01:28:12.436 --> 01:28:15.513
That the Richardsonian novel's
always setting itself up.

01:28:15.513 --> 01:28:17.776
Like it's always self-exposed,

01:28:17.776 --> 01:28:20.650
committed to a kind of self exposure.

01:28:20.650 --> 01:28:24.277
- Yeah, which is why a lot
of people don't like it.

01:28:24.277 --> 01:28:25.444
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

01:28:28.024 --> 01:28:29.958
- I was just gonna say.

01:28:29.958 --> 01:28:32.053
If on a very basic level of camp effect

01:28:32.053 --> 01:28:34.811
of something of something
like a drag performance

01:28:34.811 --> 01:28:38.038
has to do, or is contingent upon

01:28:38.038 --> 01:28:42.910
the sort of indeterminacy between
essential and natural states

01:28:42.910 --> 01:28:44.330
and perform the state.

01:28:44.330 --> 01:28:48.205
There is a sort of tie
in to your paper Sarah

01:28:48.205 --> 01:28:52.366
about the way in which every
time we think we have a frame.

01:28:52.366 --> 01:28:53.199
- Yeah.

01:28:54.568 --> 01:28:57.040
- "Castle of Otranto" be a frame of affect

01:28:57.040 --> 01:28:59.740
or a frame of character.

01:28:59.740 --> 01:29:01.955
And I think on a certain level

01:29:01.955 --> 01:29:04.828
the text is sort of
performing indeterminacies

01:29:04.828 --> 01:29:06.578
all across the board.

01:29:08.072 --> 01:29:11.822
Which perhaps has a sort-of
affected defect

01:29:15.523 --> 01:29:17.241
similar to sitting in a drag show

01:29:17.241 --> 01:29:18.748
where there is a certain sort of

01:29:18.748 --> 01:29:20.613
nervous tension that has to break.

01:29:20.613 --> 01:29:23.397
You have to become
comfortable with the fact that

01:29:23.397 --> 01:29:27.420
the figure before you is
in state of performance.

01:29:27.420 --> 01:29:29.148
Could be that they are indeterminate.

01:29:29.148 --> 01:29:29.981
- Yeah.

01:29:29.981 --> 01:29:32.314
- Text also is flowing
back and forth quickly

01:29:32.314 --> 01:29:34.030
and it doesn't really know what it is.

01:29:34.030 --> 01:29:35.531
The reader doesn't know what it is.

01:29:35.531 --> 01:29:36.475
- Yeah that seems right to me.

01:29:36.475 --> 01:29:38.437
I was thinking when you were talking Wendy

01:29:38.437 --> 01:29:41.833
that in some ways I
feel like I was saying.

01:29:41.833 --> 01:29:43.563
Because when you were quoting Spinoza

01:29:43.563 --> 01:29:45.074
on wonders, the concept of anything

01:29:45.074 --> 01:29:46.969
when the mind comes to a stand.

01:29:46.969 --> 01:29:48.242
It was like oh God I was just making

01:29:48.242 --> 01:29:49.518
the same argument over again

01:29:49.518 --> 01:29:51.076
because I was kind of saying the book

01:29:51.076 --> 01:29:52.564
makes the mind come to a stand.

01:29:52.564 --> 01:29:54.658
But I don't think I want to call it wonder

01:29:54.658 --> 01:29:57.227
or that's not trying to say that.

01:29:57.227 --> 01:30:01.394
But it's the effect of that
indeterminacy or code switching

01:30:03.170 --> 01:30:07.000
where are you experiencing
this as a suspenseful novel

01:30:07.000 --> 01:30:08.451
or are you immersed in it.

01:30:08.451 --> 01:30:11.425
Or is somebody, are the
characters in the novel

01:30:11.425 --> 01:30:15.725
following clues or are
they just experiencing

01:30:15.725 --> 01:30:17.817
these weird dream states
and hallucinations

01:30:17.817 --> 01:30:19.892
and not knowing what to do with them?

01:30:19.892 --> 01:30:21.928
- Or even in a setup with
Walpole's second preface

01:30:21.928 --> 01:30:24.022
are you experiencing
romance or a realist novel.

01:30:24.022 --> 01:30:24.855
- Yeah.

01:30:24.855 --> 01:30:25.951
- Push you back and forth.

01:30:25.951 --> 01:30:26.784
- Exactly.

01:30:26.784 --> 01:30:28.601
- Yeah I was thinking more about

01:30:28.601 --> 01:30:31.476
how the prefaces have a.

01:30:31.476 --> 01:30:33.439
I mean, I know I'm just
seeing shells everywhere now

01:30:33.439 --> 01:30:35.810
but just the fact that
there are these frames.

01:30:35.810 --> 01:30:36.838
That there are these frameworks

01:30:36.838 --> 01:30:39.618
that almost overwhelm the text itself.

01:30:39.618 --> 01:30:40.451
- Yeah.

01:30:41.450 --> 01:30:42.713
- And as Margaret Doody has argued

01:30:42.713 --> 01:30:45.130
perhaps with excessive vigor.

01:30:46.511 --> 01:30:49.756
The romance was still very much in print

01:30:49.756 --> 01:30:52.407
and alive at this time.

01:30:52.407 --> 01:30:54.404
So that switching is not between

01:30:54.404 --> 01:30:58.948
a hypothetical form
that's old and a new form.

01:30:58.948 --> 01:31:00.531
They're both there.

01:31:03.407 --> 01:31:04.757
- So this might not be surprising.

01:31:04.757 --> 01:31:06.988
But I guess I want to actually push back

01:31:06.988 --> 01:31:11.304
a little bit against this
language of indeterminacy

01:31:11.304 --> 01:31:14.367
or maybe to get more precise about

01:31:14.367 --> 01:31:15.995
what we're talking about

01:31:15.995 --> 01:31:18.426
when we're pointing at certain
things that happen in the text.

01:31:18.426 --> 01:31:21.409
So I spent a lot of time
in the last two weeks

01:31:21.409 --> 01:31:23.456
when I was teaching this,
trying to figure out

01:31:23.456 --> 01:31:27.610
what actually comes out of
the frame in that scene.

01:31:27.610 --> 01:31:30.480
Because it isn't in fact the portrait,

01:31:30.480 --> 01:31:33.796
if by the portrait you mean
the thing on the canvas.

01:31:33.796 --> 01:31:35.796
I think it's actually supposed to be

01:31:35.796 --> 01:31:37.203
the figure on the canvas.

01:31:37.203 --> 01:31:38.036
- Yeah.

01:31:38.036 --> 01:31:41.111
- Because that's the only
thing that can shake its head.

01:31:41.111 --> 01:31:44.665
Okay, so I just want to say that.

01:31:44.665 --> 01:31:46.825
So that we know what we're talking about.

01:31:46.825 --> 01:31:50.325
And similarly with the cheek of the helmet

01:31:53.809 --> 01:31:57.809
as your sort of emblem,
Sarah for this metaphor.

01:31:58.688 --> 01:32:01.089
Because that's actually metonymy.

01:32:01.089 --> 01:32:04.197
It's the cheek, the cheek is the helmet.

01:32:04.197 --> 01:32:06.155
The helmet had the cheek
only to extent that

01:32:06.155 --> 01:32:09.704
the helmet is designed to
point to the human body

01:32:09.704 --> 01:32:11.954
that it no longer contains.

01:32:13.963 --> 01:32:16.555
So those are two things,

01:32:16.555 --> 01:32:17.555
two material

01:32:23.945 --> 01:32:28.600
features of the text that
I want us to hold onto.

01:32:28.600 --> 01:32:31.333
And then I wanted to point to something

01:32:31.333 --> 01:32:35.378
that intrigued me about the Foley video.

01:32:35.378 --> 01:32:38.274
Which is that there's an interesting,

01:32:38.274 --> 01:32:41.400
a kind of interestingly naive literalism

01:32:41.400 --> 01:32:44.317
where in order to produce the sound

01:32:45.246 --> 01:32:46.731
they seem to feel they have to

01:32:46.731 --> 01:32:49.211
move their bodies in the same way.

01:32:49.211 --> 01:32:51.657
So the moment it really struck me

01:32:51.657 --> 01:32:53.340
was when the guy falls down on the floor

01:32:53.340 --> 01:32:56.741
in order to make the
sound of falling down.

01:32:56.741 --> 01:32:59.798
Whereas presumably you
could make a sound like that

01:32:59.798 --> 01:33:03.469
and we'd all sit in the
theater be like, yeah.

01:33:03.469 --> 01:33:06.300
- There are countless examples
in literature about this.

01:33:06.300 --> 01:33:09.669
This video just happened to be
the most vivid I could find.

01:33:09.669 --> 01:33:11.798
But also I had to cut,
it's five minutes long

01:33:11.798 --> 01:33:13.576
and I only had 10 minutes.

01:33:13.576 --> 01:33:16.445
I had to cut it off
before the leg of lamb.

01:33:16.445 --> 01:33:20.947
There's a big fight and they're
socking this leg of lamb.

01:33:20.947 --> 01:33:22.475
(everyone laughs)

01:33:22.475 --> 01:33:24.941
- So there we have a kind of,

01:33:24.941 --> 01:33:27.674
so it's not so much it seemed to me

01:33:27.674 --> 01:33:30.706
that we've got realism without the real.

01:33:30.706 --> 01:33:33.956
We've got the real but detached from the.

01:33:35.875 --> 01:33:36.943
- From the putative.

01:33:36.943 --> 01:33:39.328
- From one reference right.

01:33:39.328 --> 01:33:41.937
- We have two realities.

01:33:41.937 --> 01:33:44.319
- So this is all very confused

01:33:44.319 --> 01:33:45.811
but it seemed to me that what's happening

01:33:45.811 --> 01:33:48.746
when we talked about
indeterminacy and frames

01:33:48.746 --> 01:33:51.587
and we too easily slip into saying

01:33:51.587 --> 01:33:54.089
what's moving out of the picture.

01:33:54.089 --> 01:33:57.418
And the picture is moving out of the frame

01:33:57.418 --> 01:34:00.085
is a kind of de-materialization.

01:34:01.343 --> 01:34:03.502
Which we do all the time.

01:34:03.502 --> 01:34:06.581
It's a metaphor for some larger things.

01:34:06.581 --> 01:34:09.331
And then there you have a kind of

01:34:11.817 --> 01:34:15.984
naive commitment to a materiality
which would require that

01:34:17.742 --> 01:34:21.376
the sounds of the human body in motion

01:34:21.376 --> 01:34:23.836
would have to be produced by that body

01:34:23.836 --> 01:34:28.003
or version of that body in
the same kind of motion.

01:34:29.560 --> 01:34:33.643
- It's a technical problem
in the making of film.

01:34:34.574 --> 01:34:38.153
I mean in theory, all
those falls and so forth,

01:34:38.153 --> 01:34:41.508
when they were shot,
could have been recorded

01:34:41.508 --> 01:34:43.582
on the soundtrack with the dialogue.

01:34:43.582 --> 01:34:45.251
But it doesn't work

01:34:45.251 --> 01:34:48.853
because there's so much
other noise around.

01:34:48.853 --> 01:34:52.105
And so with certain Foley things

01:34:52.105 --> 01:34:55.424
there just has to be a literalness,
the click of the latch.

01:34:55.424 --> 01:34:57.734
Now they can go to a lot more work

01:34:57.734 --> 01:34:59.237
and click the latch some other way.

01:34:59.237 --> 01:35:02.741
And in fact now, much of
that is digital anyway.

01:35:02.741 --> 01:35:06.908
I mean, the real Foley is
this kind of physicality.

01:35:08.244 --> 01:35:09.744
But I think to me.

01:35:10.602 --> 01:35:12.442
- I guess if it's a simulacrum.

01:35:12.442 --> 01:35:14.442
Why is it such a kind of

01:35:17.268 --> 01:35:20.606
literal, naively literal simulacrum?

01:35:20.606 --> 01:35:23.754
- You would, if you just had the video

01:35:23.754 --> 01:35:27.004
of the two guys and all of their stuff.

01:35:28.304 --> 01:35:31.749
Assuming you would accept the
idea of the chase in the film

01:35:31.749 --> 01:35:34.015
creates a certain suspense.

01:35:34.015 --> 01:35:36.237
You would not experience
that watching these guys

01:35:36.237 --> 01:35:38.787
hammer on trash cans and fall over and go.

01:35:38.787 --> 01:35:40.076
And did you notice how the one guy

01:35:40.076 --> 01:35:42.693
running down the hall went (stomps feet)?

01:35:42.693 --> 01:35:43.907
- Yeah.

01:35:43.907 --> 01:35:44.740
- And so.

01:35:45.974 --> 01:35:48.295
- To represent distance.

01:35:48.295 --> 01:35:51.104
- Yes exactly, that's right.

01:35:51.104 --> 01:35:55.787
So I think your point's
well taken in some ways

01:35:55.787 --> 01:35:58.925
but the, I guess if
this were a longer paper

01:35:58.925 --> 01:36:03.092
I would go more into parallel
technical issues in the novel

01:36:05.666 --> 01:36:08.666
about how these effects become real.

01:36:09.828 --> 01:36:13.887
I used this blanket term about
the audio visual contract

01:36:13.887 --> 01:36:16.470
to kind of cover a lot of that.

01:36:17.863 --> 01:36:19.113
But it's, yeah.

01:36:22.210 --> 01:36:23.793
- So just thinking.

01:36:26.197 --> 01:36:27.863
So you're asking the question.

01:36:27.863 --> 01:36:31.421
Why is a problem with materiality

01:36:31.421 --> 01:36:33.921
being solved with materiality?

01:36:36.792 --> 01:36:38.873
Which is, yeah or something.

01:36:38.873 --> 01:36:39.706
Is that?

01:36:39.706 --> 01:36:42.567
- I mean I guess I just wanted to know.

01:36:42.567 --> 01:36:46.086
Does it matter for us to get clear on

01:36:46.086 --> 01:36:48.419
what emerges from the frame?

01:36:49.731 --> 01:36:50.564
- Yeah.

01:36:50.564 --> 01:36:54.927
- In our account of what is
significant about that trope

01:36:54.927 --> 01:36:56.844
or that moment or that.

01:36:57.985 --> 01:36:59.144
- And it's still mimetic

01:36:59.144 --> 01:37:00.888
is partly what you're saying isn't it?

01:37:00.888 --> 01:37:03.971
That both in the Foley and the cheeks

01:37:05.747 --> 01:37:10.083
that these are not just
objects or artificial effects

01:37:10.083 --> 01:37:14.250
but they're still kind of
bound to the body in some way.

01:37:15.253 --> 01:37:16.748
Whether it's like representationally

01:37:16.748 --> 01:37:18.124
in the figure of the portrait

01:37:18.124 --> 01:37:21.480
or that the cheeks because
they're molded on the cheeks.

01:37:21.480 --> 01:37:22.789
And I think that's really true.

01:37:22.789 --> 01:37:25.073
And I just wanted to say I
think you're totally right that

01:37:25.073 --> 01:37:27.705
I don't want it to flatten it out all into

01:37:27.705 --> 01:37:29.524
well everything's indeterminate

01:37:29.524 --> 01:37:32.429
and then at the end that
seems boring and not true.

01:37:32.429 --> 01:37:35.262
But yeah, I think we need to cling

01:37:36.507 --> 01:37:41.020
to the specificity of
these particular moments.

01:37:41.020 --> 01:37:44.854
And I would need to think harder
about exactly how they work

01:37:44.854 --> 01:37:48.437
and in the panel and
the cheek of the cask.

01:37:50.094 --> 01:37:52.608
- Part of what's coming
out of the frame is Walpole

01:37:52.608 --> 01:37:55.499
with the second preface
that is his authorship.

01:37:55.499 --> 01:37:56.332
- Yeah.

01:37:56.332 --> 01:37:57.185
- Yeah.
- Right.

01:37:57.185 --> 01:37:58.018
- His reveals.

01:37:58.018 --> 01:37:58.851
- Yeah.

01:37:59.914 --> 01:38:01.969
- And yeah it's, what was I gonna say?

01:38:01.969 --> 01:38:02.802
Oh well.

01:38:02.802 --> 01:38:03.997
- He also apparently is thinking of

01:38:03.997 --> 01:38:05.889
a particular portrait at Strawberry Hill

01:38:05.889 --> 01:38:08.458
because he mentions that too in a letter.

01:38:08.458 --> 01:38:11.521
He says when that happens,
you should think of

01:38:11.521 --> 01:38:13.575
I don't remember what the portrait is.

01:38:13.575 --> 01:38:15.278
So that would be interesting to know.

01:38:15.278 --> 01:38:17.445
Like what specific images.

01:38:18.970 --> 01:38:20.220
- To me though.

01:38:21.233 --> 01:38:23.566
What is being imitated here?

01:38:24.557 --> 01:38:27.576
To me this book is like sci-fi.

01:38:27.576 --> 01:38:31.240
It's using the techniques
that we're familiar with

01:38:31.240 --> 01:38:33.500
from the realist novel.

01:38:33.500 --> 01:38:36.667
But what's being imitated isn't there.

01:38:38.450 --> 01:38:39.995
And this is part of the critique

01:38:39.995 --> 01:38:43.226
of the so-called, the
Fielding Richardson novel.

01:38:43.226 --> 01:38:45.803
That's not there either.

01:38:45.803 --> 01:38:48.924
But he's calling attention
to the not-thereness

01:38:48.924 --> 01:38:51.678
of the objects being represented.

01:38:51.678 --> 01:38:55.345
It's I think, a brilliant
treatice on mimesis

01:38:57.571 --> 01:38:59.032
which is part of what I'm trying to get at

01:38:59.032 --> 01:39:01.446
with the Foley effects.

01:39:01.446 --> 01:39:03.015
- Just to be dumb for a second.

01:39:03.015 --> 01:39:05.307
I mean the Richardson Fielding
thing kind of is there

01:39:05.307 --> 01:39:09.474
in the sense that if you
sort of strip away all the

01:39:10.724 --> 01:39:12.789
kind of Medieval stuff, it's basically

01:39:12.789 --> 01:39:16.129
just a family having a row about.

01:39:16.129 --> 01:39:18.296
- And he makes a clue that

01:39:19.519 --> 01:39:24.494
he's keeping the psychological
realism of a modern author.

01:39:24.494 --> 01:39:28.494
But he's just entertaining
with a magical world.

01:39:30.909 --> 01:39:33.400
- Yeah his term is expanded fantasy.

01:39:33.400 --> 01:39:36.692
There's not enough
fantasy in those novels.

01:39:36.692 --> 01:39:38.189
- Also I wanted to ask you Sophie

01:39:38.189 --> 01:39:40.170
a question about this.

01:39:40.170 --> 01:39:42.087
As you see it out here. (indistinct)

01:39:49.801 --> 01:39:53.051
It seems like Walpole is about
going on and encompasses

01:39:55.484 --> 01:39:57.111
- Yeah.

01:39:57.111 --> 01:39:59.810
- And then in the ways
of the novel itself.

01:39:59.810 --> 01:40:01.893
By saying something like.

01:40:03.416 --> 01:40:07.186
The credulity of this
world of the Middle Ages.

01:40:07.186 --> 01:40:09.769
He uses the whole supernatural apparatus.

01:40:11.635 --> 01:40:15.290
And then in the second preface
he goes away from that.

01:40:15.290 --> 01:40:19.457
It seems like maybe there's
a status of internally

01:40:20.646 --> 01:40:23.329
the novel had some sort of
contractual relationship

01:40:23.329 --> 01:40:26.167
with the reader of what
we're supposed to believe

01:40:26.167 --> 01:40:27.584
to transform you.

01:40:29.670 --> 01:40:31.017
- Over the course of the book?

01:40:31.017 --> 01:40:31.948
- Yeah, over the course.

01:40:31.948 --> 01:40:33.095
- The publications of the book.

01:40:33.095 --> 01:40:33.928
- Exactly.

01:40:33.928 --> 01:40:36.721
- Scott takes that up in his preface

01:40:36.721 --> 01:40:39.689
and tries to treat this
as an historical novel

01:40:39.689 --> 01:40:41.022
to make it work.

01:40:47.152 --> 01:40:49.151
- The reason I'm hesitating is that

01:40:49.151 --> 01:40:51.484
I am interested in something

01:40:53.279 --> 01:40:54.776
that A, I haven fully thought through

01:40:54.776 --> 01:40:56.933
but B, I think is a little subtle.

01:40:56.933 --> 01:40:59.016
Which is for me, which is

01:41:00.815 --> 01:41:03.089
the obvious move to make about the Gothic

01:41:03.089 --> 01:41:05.377
and all this kind of stuff and fantasy

01:41:05.377 --> 01:41:08.457
and that video and everything.

01:41:08.457 --> 01:41:13.334
Is to say, there's just to
be really simple about it.

01:41:13.334 --> 01:41:16.247
There's way too much
empirical stuff going on.

01:41:16.247 --> 01:41:19.557
There's way too much real,
there's way too much material.

01:41:19.557 --> 01:41:22.687
There's way too much stuff
that's supposed to be

01:41:22.687 --> 01:41:25.707
like the thing it's imitating.

01:41:25.707 --> 01:41:28.180
So we should jump the other way

01:41:28.180 --> 01:41:30.763
and we should reinstate belief.

01:41:32.838 --> 01:41:36.421
We should prioritize
belief over knowledge.

01:41:38.197 --> 01:41:42.604
We should prioritize
credulity over skepticism.

01:41:42.604 --> 01:41:43.854
Blah blah blah.

01:41:45.310 --> 01:41:48.912
And what seems uninteresting
to me about that solution

01:41:48.912 --> 01:41:52.827
is that it isn't true and it doesn't work.

01:41:52.827 --> 01:41:55.660
(audience laughs)

01:42:00.465 --> 01:42:04.828
This is sort of where I fall
off the edge of the world.

01:42:04.828 --> 01:42:07.014
I don't really have that
much more to say about that

01:42:07.014 --> 01:42:08.015
because I haven't thought it through.

01:42:08.015 --> 01:42:11.598
But I think that the
Gothic isn't proposing

01:42:12.981 --> 01:42:15.564
a return to the credulous
and the superstitious

01:42:15.564 --> 01:42:19.731
or the immaterial or the
non-real, the counter empirical

01:42:23.025 --> 01:42:26.858
as a sort of curative
or antidote to the real.

01:42:28.211 --> 01:42:31.889
I don't think, even though
it makes that gesture.

01:42:31.889 --> 01:42:33.560
It feels to me.

01:42:33.560 --> 01:42:36.643
I've never thought about camp before.

01:42:37.552 --> 01:42:41.134
So I'm very, very beginner mind about it.

01:42:41.134 --> 01:42:45.773
But it feels interesting
to me in sort of this,

01:42:45.773 --> 01:42:48.690
the relation of belief to knowledge

01:42:50.205 --> 01:42:53.447
or of credulity to skepticism.

01:42:53.447 --> 01:42:56.184
Feels similar to me in the Gothic

01:42:56.184 --> 01:43:00.351
as the relation of camp
to heterosexual matrimony.

01:43:04.373 --> 01:43:05.706
It is a solution

01:43:10.162 --> 01:43:14.464
that does all the things
that you said, somehow.

01:43:14.464 --> 01:43:16.810
But yeah, I have to think more about it.

01:43:16.810 --> 01:43:20.310
But the note I would like to sound is that

01:43:23.479 --> 01:43:26.979
it isn't, I'm trying to think through the.

01:43:29.409 --> 01:43:30.906
And this is where all the stuff about

01:43:30.906 --> 01:43:32.823
involuntary obligation.

01:43:37.550 --> 01:43:40.772
What was the other thing I talked about?

01:43:40.772 --> 01:43:44.404
Like, okay I can't remember but anyway.

01:43:44.404 --> 01:43:46.141
That's where that all comes in.

01:43:46.141 --> 01:43:49.539
Because I'm sort of trying
to think through this idea.

01:43:49.539 --> 01:43:50.988
Basically I'm just trying
to think through the idea

01:43:50.988 --> 01:43:53.717
that we don't have to continue to chew on

01:43:53.717 --> 01:43:58.001
the kind of remnants of
credulity versus skepticism

01:43:58.001 --> 01:44:00.434
and belief versus knowledge
and all that stuff.

01:44:00.434 --> 01:44:04.006
And yet we're dealing
with here's the archive.

01:44:04.006 --> 01:44:05.714
- Yeah and over time you have to really

01:44:05.714 --> 01:44:07.718
just willing suspension at this point.

01:44:07.718 --> 01:44:09.845
- Right, right, right.

01:44:09.845 --> 01:44:11.375
- Because what happens,
it's taken for granted.

01:44:11.375 --> 01:44:13.712
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:44:13.712 --> 01:44:15.796
And actually on that point.

01:44:15.796 --> 01:44:19.546
I would say that Coleridge
is in fact putting his.

01:44:19.546 --> 01:44:21.322
It's not like Coleridge thinks up

01:44:21.322 --> 01:44:23.387
the willing suspension
of disbelief per se.

01:44:23.387 --> 01:44:27.009
But he puts his finger,
or he identifies something

01:44:27.009 --> 01:44:29.806
that seems to me, he gives it a name

01:44:29.806 --> 01:44:32.793
but it doesn't seem to me
that that's the origin moment

01:44:32.793 --> 01:44:36.793
for that particular
intellectual cognitive move.

01:44:38.734 --> 01:44:41.379
That's all of it, yeah.

01:44:41.379 --> 01:44:42.784
- And where does Walpole's word fancy

01:44:42.784 --> 01:44:45.451
fit into this belief skepticism.

01:44:46.810 --> 01:44:47.756
- That's a good question.

01:44:47.756 --> 01:44:49.404
So how does he use the word fancy?

01:44:49.404 --> 01:44:51.117
- He does, that's precisely his word.

01:44:51.117 --> 01:44:52.609
- No, how?

01:44:52.609 --> 01:44:53.658
Can you read the quote?

01:44:53.658 --> 01:44:54.741
- Yeah I can.

01:44:56.429 --> 01:44:58.510
"Invention has not been wanting

01:44:58.510 --> 01:45:01.319
"but the great resources of
fancy have been damned up

01:45:01.319 --> 01:45:04.642
"by a strict adherence to common life."

01:45:04.642 --> 01:45:06.089
- That's a really good question.

01:45:06.089 --> 01:45:09.054
I don't know, I bet I could
give you a good answer to that

01:45:09.054 --> 01:45:10.934
but I definitely can't do it right now.

01:45:10.934 --> 01:45:12.574
(everyone laughs)

01:45:12.574 --> 01:45:13.522
- Well, I mean.

01:45:13.522 --> 01:45:14.355
- Do you know?

01:45:14.355 --> 01:45:15.636
- This is a moment of methodism.

01:45:15.636 --> 01:45:18.863
It's a continuing battle
against Catholicism.

01:45:18.863 --> 01:45:19.696
- Yeah.

01:45:19.696 --> 01:45:20.630
- In the Anglican Church.

01:45:20.630 --> 01:45:21.463
- Yeah.

01:45:21.463 --> 01:45:25.778
- I think there are a lot of
phantasms of fancy out there.

01:45:25.778 --> 01:45:26.611
- Yeah.

01:45:26.611 --> 01:45:28.550
Well I mean, I guess part of what

01:45:28.550 --> 01:45:30.233
I was trying to say about his dad

01:45:30.233 --> 01:45:34.400
is that he was all, he was
sort of pretty invested in

01:45:37.853 --> 01:45:40.216
making fancy more safe or something

01:45:40.216 --> 01:45:42.799
by sort of limiting its impact.

01:45:46.195 --> 01:45:48.733
- Is your Hoadly the guy Hogarth painted?

01:45:48.733 --> 01:45:50.233
- Yes, yeah, yeah.

01:45:52.862 --> 01:45:55.967
- Shall we continue the
conversation informally.

01:45:55.967 --> 01:45:59.033
And thank all the panelists
today and this morning

01:45:59.033 --> 01:46:01.893
for a wonderful, wonderful.

01:46:01.893 --> 01:46:04.893
(audience clapping)

